window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-phy-oscillations","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Oscillations · Practice","chapter":"Oscillations","subject":"Physics","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":1,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"The function $f(t) = \\sqrt{3}\\sin\\omega t + \\cos\\omega t$ is equivalent to which of the following?","image":"","options":["$2\\sin(\\omega t + \\frac{\\pi}{6})$","$2\\sin(\\omega t + \\frac{\\pi}{3})$","$4\\sin(\\omega t + \\frac{\\pi}{6})$","$2\\cos(\\omega t + \\frac{\\pi}{6})$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Compare with $A\\sin\\omega t + B\\cos\\omega t$: here $A = \\sqrt{3}$ and $B = 1$.\nAmplitude: $D = \\sqrt{3 + 1} = 2$.\nPhase: $\\phi = \\tan^{-1}(\\frac{B}{A}) = \\tan^{-1}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$.\nSo $f(t) = 2\\sin(\\omega t + \\frac{\\pi}{6})$.\nOption (2) wrongly uses $\\tan^{-1}(\\frac{A}{B})$.\nOption (4) expands to $\\sqrt{3}\\cos\\omega t - \\sin\\omega t$, which is a different function.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":2,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A uniform rod of length 1.5 m swings in a vertical plane about a horizontal axis through one of its ends, with small amplitude. Use the general result $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{I}{mgl}}$, where l is the distance of the centre of mass from the axis. The moment of inertia of the rod about this end is $\\frac{mL^2}{3}$. What is the period of oscillation? (g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["1.99 s","2.43 s","1.72 s","1.40 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: L = 1.5 m, $I = \\frac{mL^2}{3}$, and the centre of mass is at $l = \\frac{L}{2}$ = 0.75 m.\nFormula: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{I}{mgl}} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{mL^2/3}{mg(L/2)}} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{2L}{3g}}$.\nSubstituting: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\times 1.5}{30}} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{0.1}$.\nSo T ≈ 1.99 s.\nTreating the rod as a simple pendulum of length L wrongly gives 2.43 s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":3,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A simple pendulum is displaced by a small angle $\\theta$ from the vertical and released. Which force provides the restoring torque about the point of support?","image":"","options":["The tension T in the string","The component $mg\\cos\\theta$ along the string","The component $mg\\sin\\theta$ perpendicular to the string","The net radial force $T - mg\\cos\\theta$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Only two forces act on the bob: tension T along the string and weight mg.\nResolve mg into $mg\\cos\\theta$ along the string and $mg\\sin\\theta$ perpendicular to it.\nTension and $mg\\cos\\theta$ act along the string, through the support, so their torque about the support is zero. The net radial force $T - mg\\cos\\theta$ only supplies the centripetal force.\nThe tangential component $mg\\sin\\theta$ gives torque $\\tau = -L(mg\\sin\\theta)$, which tends to reduce $\\theta$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":4,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle executes SHM $x = A\\cos(\\omega t + \\phi)$ with $\\omega = \\pi$ rad/s. At t = 0, it is at x = −2 cm and moving in the +x direction with speed $2\\pi\\sqrt{3}$ cm/s. After how much time does it first reach the extreme position x = +A?","image":"","options":["0.333 s","0.5 s","0.667 s","1.333 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $A\\cos\\phi = -2$ and $-A\\pi\\sin\\phi = 2\\pi\\sqrt{3}$, so $A\\sin\\phi = -2\\sqrt{3}$.\nAmplitude: $A = \\sqrt{4 + 12} = 4$ cm.\nPhase: $\\cos\\phi = -\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\sin\\phi < 0$, so $\\phi = -\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$.\nThe particle reaches x = +A when the phase first becomes 0: $\\pi t - \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = 0$.\nSo t = 2/3 s ≈ 0.667 s.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":5,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"For a particle of mass $m$ executing SHM under the force law $F = -kx$, match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Spring constant $k$\n(B) Angular frequency $\\omega$\n(C) Time period $T$\n(D) Frequency $\\nu$\nList-II:\n(I) $\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}}$\n(II) $m\\omega^2$\n(III) $2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{m}{k}}$\n(IV) $\\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}}$","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Start from $F = ma = -m\\omega^2 x$ and compare with $F = -kx$.\n(A) Comparing gives $k = m\\omega^2$ → II.\n(B) Rearranging gives $\\omega = \\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}}$ → IV.\n(C) $T = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{m}{k}}$ → III.\n(D) $\\nu = \\frac{1}{T} = \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}}$ → I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":6,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A quartz crystal in a watch vibrates with a period of $2.5\\ \\mu s$. How many complete vibrations does it make in 3 ms?","image":"","options":["120","1200","12000","7.5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $T = 2.5\\ \\mu s = 2.5\\times10^{-6}$ s and time interval $t = 3\\times10^{-3}$ s.\nFormula: frequency $\\nu = \\frac{1}{T}$, and number of vibrations $N = \\nu t = \\frac{t}{T}$.\nSubstituting: $\\nu = 4\\times10^{5}$ Hz, so $N = \\frac{3\\times10^{-3}}{2.5\\times10^{-6}}$.\nSo N = 1200 vibrations.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":7,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle executes simple harmonic motion with time period $T$. With what period does its potential energy vary with time?","image":"","options":["$T/4$","$T/2$","$T$","$2T$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Potential energy is $U = \\frac{1}{2}kA^2\\cos^2(\\omega t+\\phi)$.\nThe function $\\cos^2$ repeats when its argument changes by $\\pi$, not $2\\pi$. This happens in time $T/2$.\nPhysically, U is maximum at both extremes, $x = +A$ and $x = -A$. Both are reached in one period, so U peaks twice per cycle.\nOption (3), $T$, is the period of the displacement, not of the energy.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":8,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle executes SHM described by $x = A\\cos(\\omega t + \\phi)$ with $\\omega$ = 2 rad/s. At t = 0, its position is 1 cm and its velocity is $-2\\sqrt{3}$ cm/s. What are the amplitude A and the phase constant $\\phi$?","image":"","options":["A = 2 cm, $\\phi = -\\frac{\\pi}{3}$","A = 2 cm, $\\phi = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$","A = $\\sqrt{3}$ cm, $\\phi = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$","A = 4 cm, $\\phi = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"At t = 0: $x_0 = A\\cos\\phi = 1$ and $v_0 = -A\\omega\\sin\\phi = -2\\sqrt{3}$, so $A\\sin\\phi = \\sqrt{3}$.\nAmplitude: $A = \\sqrt{1^2 + 3} = 2$ cm.\nPhase: $\\cos\\phi = \\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\sin\\phi = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, so $\\phi = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$.\n$\\phi = -\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ would make the initial velocity positive, so it is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":9,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle of mass m performs SHM $x = A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)$. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Maximum speed\n(B) Maximum acceleration\n(C) Total mechanical energy\n(D) Period of variation of potential energy\nList-II\n(i) $\\frac{1}{2}m\\omega^2A^2$\n(ii) $\\omega A$\n(iii) $\\omega^2 A$\n(iv) $\\frac{\\pi}{\\omega}$","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Velocity is $v = -\\omega A\\sin(\\omega t+\\phi)$, so the maximum speed is $\\omega A$ → (ii).\n(B) Acceleration is $a = -\\omega^2 x$, so its maximum is $\\omega^2 A$ → (iii).\n(C) $E = \\frac{1}{2}kA^2$ with $k = m\\omega^2$, so $E = \\frac{1}{2}m\\omega^2A^2$ → (i).\n(D) Potential energy repeats after $T/2 = \\frac{\\pi}{\\omega}$ → (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":10,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"For each SHM in List-I, the reference particle moves anticlockwise on a circle of radius 3 cm with angular speed 2 rad/s. Match each SHM with the angle that the reference particle's radius vector makes with the positive x-axis at $t = 0$ (List-II). Here x is in cm and t is in s.\nList-I:\n(A) $x = 3\\sin(2t + \\pi/6)$\n(B) $x = -3\\cos(2t + \\pi/6)$\n(C) $x = -3\\sin(2t - \\pi/6)$\n(D) $x = 3\\cos(\\pi/6 - 2t)$\nList-II:\n(I) $60^\\circ$\n(II) $210^\\circ$\n(III) $300^\\circ$\n(IV) $330^\\circ$","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Write each SHM in the form $3\\cos(2t + \\phi)$. Then $\\phi$ is the initial angle on the reference circle.\n(A) $\\sin\\theta = \\cos(\\theta - \\pi/2)$, so $\\phi = \\pi/6 - \\pi/2 = -\\pi/3$, i.e. 300° → III.\n(B) $-\\cos\\theta = \\cos(\\theta + \\pi)$, so $\\phi = 7\\pi/6$, i.e. 210° → II.\n(C) $-\\sin\\theta = \\cos(\\theta + \\pi/2)$, so $\\phi = -\\pi/6 + \\pi/2 = \\pi/3$, i.e. 60° → I.\n(D) Cosine is even, so this equals $3\\cos(2t - \\pi/6)$, giving $\\phi = -\\pi/6$, i.e. 330° → IV.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":11,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"The displacement of a particle varies with time according to the relation $y = a\\sin\\omega t + b\\cos\\omega t$.","image":"","options":["The motion is oscillatory but not S.H.M.","The motion is S.H.M. with amplitude $a + b$.","The motion is S.H.M. with amplitude $a^2 + b^2$.","The motion is S.H.M. with amplitude $\\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Let $a = A\\cos\\phi$ and $b = A\\sin\\phi$.\nThen $y = A(\\sin\\omega t\\cos\\phi + \\cos\\omega t\\sin\\phi) = A\\sin(\\omega t + \\phi)$. This is a single SHM.\nAmplitude: $A = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$, with $\\tan\\phi = b/a$.\nThe amplitude is not $a + b$, because the two components are $90^\\circ$ out of phase. The value $a^2 + b^2$ is not even dimensionally a length.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 14 (MCQ Q14.6)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":12,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle revolves anticlockwise on a circle of radius 5 cm with a period of 2 s. At t = 0, its position vector makes an angle of $30^\\circ$ with the +x axis. What is the velocity of its x-projection at t = 0.5 s?","image":"","options":["−13.6 cm/s","+13.6 cm/s","−7.85 cm/s","+7.85 cm/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: A = 5 cm, $\\omega = \\frac{2\\pi}{2} = \\pi$ rad/s, $\\phi = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$\nFormula: $v = -\\omega A\\sin(\\omega t + \\phi)$\nSubstituting: $v = -5\\pi\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = -5\\pi\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{6} = -5\\pi \\times 0.866$\nSo v = −13.6 cm/s. The negative sign is expected: at $120^\\circ$, P moving anticlockwise heads towards −x.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":13,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Every oscillatory motion is periodic.\nStatement II: Uniform circular motion is an oscillatory motion because it repeats after a fixed interval.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: oscillation is a to-and-fro motion about a mean position that repeats at regular intervals, so it is periodic.\nStatement II is incorrect: NCERT states that circular motion is periodic but not oscillatory.\nThere is no to-and-fro motion about an equilibrium point in circular motion. Not every periodic motion is oscillatory.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":14,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For a particle in SHM of amplitude A and angular frequency $\\omega$, the speed equals $\\omega A$ when the particle passes through the mean position.\nStatement II: The magnitude of acceleration of this particle equals $\\omega^2 A$ when it passes through the mean position.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. From $v = \\omega\\sqrt{A^2 - x^2}$, setting $x = 0$ gives $v = \\omega A$, which is the maximum speed.\nStatement II is incorrect. Since $a = -\\omega^2 x$, the acceleration is zero at $x = 0$. The magnitude $\\omega^2 A$ is reached only at the extremes $x = \\pm A$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":15,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A block of mass 0.5 kg is attached to a horizontal spring of force constant 200 N m$^{-1}$ on a frictionless surface. The block is pulled a little from its equilibrium position and released. What is the period of its oscillation?","image":"","options":["0.314 s","0.157 s","0.628 s","3.14 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 0.5 kg, k = 200 N m$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{m}{k}}$\nSubstituting: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{0.5}{200}} = 2\\pi \\times 0.05$\nSo T = 0.314 s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":16,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A 2 kg block is attached to a spring of spring constant $200\\ \\text{N m}^{-1}$ on a smooth horizontal floor. At $t = 0$ it is at $x = +3$ cm from equilibrium and moving with velocity $+0.4\\ \\text{m s}^{-1}$. What is its kinetic energy when it passes through $x = -4$ cm?","image":"","options":["0.16 J","0.25 J","0.09 J","0.07 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $m = 2$ kg, $k = 200\\ \\text{N m}^{-1}$, $x_0 = 0.03$ m, $v_0 = 0.4\\ \\text{m s}^{-1}$.\nTotal energy from the initial conditions: $E = \\frac{1}{2}(200)(0.03)^2 + \\frac{1}{2}(2)(0.4)^2 = 0.09 + 0.16 = 0.25$ J. This also gives $A = 5$ cm.\nPotential energy at $x = -0.04$ m: $U = \\frac{1}{2}(200)(0.04)^2 = 0.16$ J. The sign of x does not matter because U depends on $x^2$.\nSo K = 0.25 − 0.16 = 0.09 J.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":17,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The frequency of a periodic motion need not be an integer","1 Hz is equal to one oscillation per second, i.e., $1\\ s^{-1}$","The voltage across a capacitor in an AC circuit can serve as a displacement variable","The period of a periodic motion is the largest interval of time after which the motion repeats"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: frequency $\\nu = 1/T$ can be any positive number, for example 1.25 Hz.\n(2) Correct: 1 Hz = 1 oscillation per second = $1\\ s^{-1}$.\n(3) Correct: in this chapter, displacement means the change with time of any physical quantity, such as voltage or pressure.\n(4) Incorrect: the period is the smallest interval after which the motion repeats. The motion also repeats after nT for any integer n, so there is no largest such interval.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":18,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A small ball resting at the bottom of a hemispherical bowl oscillates when displaced slightly. A small ball balanced on top of an inverted bowl does not oscillate when displaced slightly.\nStatement II: For oscillations to occur about an equilibrium position, a small displacement must bring into play a force that tends to return the body to that position.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true, and Statement II correctly explains Statement I","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II is true. NCERT says that when a body is displaced slightly from equilibrium, a force arises that tries to bring it back, and this gives rise to oscillations.\nStatement I is true. At the bottom of a bowl, gravity pulls a displaced ball back towards the lowest point, so it oscillates. On top of an inverted bowl, the gravity component pushes the ball further away, so no oscillation occurs.\nThe difference between the two cases is exactly the presence or absence of a restoring force, so II explains I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":19,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about a simple pendulum (bob of mass m, string of length L) is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The tangential component $mg\\sin\\theta$ provides the restoring torque about the support.","The motion is simple harmonic for any angular amplitude, since the restoring torque always opposes the displacement.","The net radial force $T - mg\\cos\\theta$ supplies the centripetal force and produces no torque about the support.","The moment of inertia of the system about the support is $mL^2$ because the string is massless."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Options (1), (3) and (4) are all NCERT statements, so they are correct.\nOption (2) is incorrect. The restoring torque is $-mgL\\sin\\theta$, which is proportional to $\\sin\\theta$, not to $\\theta$.\nOnly for small $\\theta$ can we use $\\sin\\theta \\approx \\theta$, which makes the angular acceleration proportional to $-\\theta$.\nA torque that merely opposes the displacement is not enough for SHM; it must be proportional to the displacement.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":20,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"For a particle in simple harmonic motion, the displacement–time, velocity–time and acceleration–time graphs all have the same period. With respect to the displacement graph, the acceleration graph differs in phase by","image":"","options":["zero","$\\frac{\\pi}{2}$","$\\pi$","$\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key relation: from $a(t) = -\\omega^2 x(t)$, acceleration always has the sign opposite to displacement.\nA negative sign in front of a sinusoid is equivalent to a phase shift of $\\pi$, so the acceleration plot differs from the displacement plot by $\\pi$.\nThe tempting option $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ is the phase difference of velocity, not acceleration.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":21,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A block of mass 2 kg rests on a frictionless horizontal surface. It is connected between two identical springs, each of force constant 100 N m$^{-1}$, whose other ends are fixed to rigid walls on either side. Both springs are unstretched at equilibrium. The block is displaced slightly along the line of the springs and released. Find the period of oscillation.","image":"","options":["0.628 s","0.888 s","0.444 s","1.256 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 2 kg, k = 100 N m$^{-1}$ for each spring.\nFor a displacement x, one spring stretches and the other compresses by x. Both forces point towards the mean position, so F = −2kx.\nFormula: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{m}{2k}}$\nSubstituting: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{200}} = 2\\pi \\times 0.1$\nSo T = 0.628 s. The value 0.888 s comes from wrongly using only one spring.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":22,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Damping\n(b) Forced oscillation\n(c) Wave\n(d) Restoring force\nList-II\n(i) Oscillation kept going by an external periodic agency\n(ii) Friction and other dissipative causes bring the oscillating body to rest at equilibrium\n(iii) Collective oscillation of the coupled constituents of a medium\n(iv) Comes into play on small displacement and tends to bring the body back to equilibrium","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Damping: dissipative causes such as friction make oscillations die out, so (a)-(ii).\n(b) Forced oscillation: an external periodic agency keeps the body oscillating, so (b)-(i).\n(c) Wave: the collective oscillations of coupled oscillators in a medium, so (c)-(iii).\n(d) Restoring force: it acts towards equilibrium after a small displacement, so (d)-(iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":23,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A body undergoes a periodic motion of period T. After which of the following time intervals is the body certain to be back in exactly the same state of motion?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{T}{2}$","$3T$","$1.5T$","$\\frac{T}{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"T is the least interval after which the motion repeats. So the motion also repeats after nT, where n is an integer.\n3T is an integer multiple of T, so the state repeats.\nT/2, 1.5T and T/3 are not integer multiples of T. Repetition at these times is not guaranteed for a general periodic motion.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":24,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle in SHM has a speed of 0.3 m/s when its displacement is 4 cm, and a speed of 0.4 m/s when its displacement is 3 cm. What is the maximum acceleration of the particle?","image":"","options":["5 m/s$^2$","0.5 m/s$^2$","50 m/s$^2$","2.5 m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $v_1 = 0.3$ m/s at $x_1 = 0.04$ m, and $v_2 = 0.4$ m/s at $x_2 = 0.03$ m.\nFormula: $v^2 = \\omega^2(A^2 - x^2)$, which gives two equations.\nSubtracting the two equations: $0.16 - 0.09 = \\omega^2(0.0016 - 0.0009)$, so $\\omega^2 = 100$ and $\\omega = 10$ rad/s.\nThen $A^2 = 0.0016 + \\frac{0.09}{100} = 0.0025$, so $A = 0.05$ m.\nSo $a_{max} = \\omega^2 A = 100 \\times 0.05 = 5$ m/s$^2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":25,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about oscillatory motion as described in NCERT:\n(i) When a body is at its equilibrium position, no net external force acts on it.\n(ii) When a body is slightly displaced from equilibrium, the force that arises pushes it further away from the equilibrium point.\n(iii) Low-frequency to and fro motion is usually called oscillation, and high-frequency motion is usually called vibration.\n(iv) Damping due to friction can make a body oscillate forever. Without damping, an external periodic agency is needed.\nWhich of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i) and (ii) only","(i) and (iii) only","(ii) and (iv) only","(i), (iii) and (iv) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: at the equilibrium position the net external force is zero. A body left there at rest stays there.\n(ii) Incorrect: the force that arises is a restoring force. It tries to bring the body back towards equilibrium, not away from it.\n(iii) Correct: a tree branch (low frequency) oscillates, while an instrument string (high frequency) vibrates.\n(iv) Incorrect: damping makes oscillating bodies eventually come to rest. An external periodic agency is needed to keep them oscillating despite damping.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":26,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle P moves anticlockwise with constant angular speed $\\omega$ on a circle of radius $A$ centred at the origin O. At $t = 0$, OP makes an angle $\\phi$ with the positive x-axis. Which statement correctly describes the motion of the foot of the perpendicular from P onto the y-axis?","image":"","options":["It is $y = A\\sin(\\omega t + \\phi)$, an SHM with the same amplitude as the x-projection but differing from it in phase by $\\pi/2$","It is $y = A\\sin(\\omega t + \\phi)$, which is not SHM because SHM must be written as a cosine function","It is $y = A\\cos(\\omega t + \\phi)$, identical in amplitude and phase to the x-projection","It is $y = A\\sin(\\omega t + \\phi)$, in the same phase as the x-projection but with a different amplitude"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"At time t, OP makes an angle $(\\omega t + \\phi)$ with the +x axis, so the x-projection is $x = A\\cos(\\omega t + \\phi)$.\nThe y-component of OP is $A\\sin(\\omega t + \\phi)$. This is a sinusoidal function of time, so it is also SHM.\nIts amplitude is the same, A. Since $\\sin\\theta = \\cos(\\theta - \\pi/2)$, it differs in phase from the x-projection by $\\pi/2$.\nOption (2) fails because the sine and cosine forms of SHM are equivalent.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":27,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Every oscillatory motion is periodic.\nStatement II: Uniform circular motion is periodic but not oscillatory.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. An oscillation is a to and fro motion about a mean position that repeats at regular intervals, so it is always periodic.\nStatement II is correct. A body in uniform circular motion repeats its motion every revolution. However, it does not move to and fro about an equilibrium point, so it is periodic but not oscillatory.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":28,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about simple harmonic motion are correct?\n(i) The restoring force is always directed towards the mean position.\n(ii) The period of a linear harmonic oscillator increases when its amplitude is increased.\n(iii) The velocity–time graph differs in phase from the displacement–time graph by $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n(iv) Both kinetic energy and potential energy reach their peak values twice in each period.\n(v) A simple pendulum with a massless string executes SHM for any angular amplitude.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: F = –kx always points towards the mean position.\n(ii) Incorrect: the period $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{m/k}$ does not depend on amplitude.\n(iii) Correct: NCERT states that the velocity plot differs in phase by π/2 and the acceleration plot by π.\n(iv) Correct: K and U each have period T/2, so each peaks twice per cycle.\n(v) Incorrect: the motion is SHM only for small θ, where sin θ ≈ θ.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":29,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A block of mass 2 kg attached to a spring of spring constant 200 N m$^{-1}$ oscillates on a frictionless horizontal surface with amplitude 5 cm. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Angular frequency\n(B) Maximum speed\n(C) Maximum acceleration\n(D) Total mechanical energy\nList-II:\n(i) 0.25 J\n(ii) 5 m s$^{-2}$\n(iii) 0.5 m s$^{-1}$\n(iv) 10 rad s$^{-1}$","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i","A-i, B-iii, C-ii, D-iv","A-iv, B-iii, C-i, D-ii","A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: m = 2 kg, k = 200 N m$^{-1}$, A = 0.05 m.\n(A) $\\omega = \\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}} = \\sqrt{100} = 10$ rad s$^{-1}$ → (iv).\n(B) $v_m = \\omega A = 10 \\times 0.05 = 0.5$ m s$^{-1}$ → (iii).\n(C) $a_m = \\omega^2 A = 100 \\times 0.05 = 5$ m s$^{-2}$ → (ii).\n(D) $E = \\frac{1}{2}kA^2 = \\frac{1}{2}(200)(0.0025) = 0.25$ J → (i).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":30,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A simple pendulum of length L oscillates with angular amplitude $\\theta_0$. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) At an extreme position, the tension in the string equals $mg\\cos\\theta_0$.\n(ii) At the mean position, the tension in the string equals mg.\n(iii) At the mean position, the tangential acceleration of the bob is zero.\n(iv) For $\\theta$ = 20°, $\\sin\\theta$ differs from $\\theta$ in radians by only about 2%.\n(v) For small oscillations, doubling the length doubles the period.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: at an extreme v = 0, so the radial acceleration is zero and $T - mg\\cos\\theta_0 = 0$.\n(ii) Incorrect: at the mean position the bob moves fastest, so $T = mg + \\frac{mv^2}{L} > mg$.\n(iii) Correct: the tangential force $mg\\sin\\theta$ is zero at $\\theta = 0$.\n(iv) Correct: sin 20° = 0.342 and 20° = 0.349 rad, a difference of about 2%.\n(v) Incorrect: $T \\propto \\sqrt{L}$, so the period becomes $\\sqrt{2}$ times.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":31,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle executes simple harmonic motion with a period of 0.8 s. What is the period with which its kinetic energy varies?","image":"","options":["0.4 s","0.8 s","1.6 s","0.2 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: period of SHM, T = 0.8 s.\nFormula: $K = \\frac{1}{2}kA^2\\sin^2(\\omega t+\\phi)$. Kinetic energy depends on $v^2$, so the sign of v does not matter.\nBecause of this, K repeats after T/2.\nSubstituting: period of K = 0.8/2 = 0.4 s.\nThe tempting option 0.8 s is the period of the displacement, not of the energy.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":32,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following acceleration–displacement relations describe simple harmonic motion? (all constants in SI units)\n(i) $a = -16x$\n(ii) $a = 16x$\n(iii) $a = -16x^2$\n(iv) $a = -16(x-3)$\n(v) $a = -16x^3$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"SHM requires a linear restoring relation of the form $a = -\\omega^2(\\text{displacement from mean position})$.\n(i) Yes: SHM about x = 0 with $\\omega$ = 4 rad/s.\n(ii) No: the acceleration points away from the mean position, so there is no restoring action.\n(iii) No: the acceleration is not linear in x, and it does not even change sign with x.\n(iv) Yes: put X = x − 3 to get $a = -16X$. This is SHM about x = 3.\n(v) No: the relation is non-linear, so the motion is a non-linear oscillation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":33,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Two blocks, each of mass 1 kg, are attached to the two ends of a light spring of force constant 200 N/m. The system rests on a frictionless horizontal floor. Each block is pulled outward by an equal force, and both are then released together. What is the period of the resulting oscillation?","image":"","options":["0.444 s","0.628 s","0.314 s","0.222 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: No external horizontal force acts, so the centre of the spring (the centre of mass) stays at rest. Each block oscillates as if fixed to a half-spring.\nStep 2: A half of the spring has force constant 2k = 400 N/m.\nFormula: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{m}{2k}}$\nSubstituting: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{400}} = 2\\pi(0.05)$\nSo T ≈ 0.314 s. The value 0.444 s wrongly treats one end as clamped.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":34,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A simple pendulum of length 2.5 m oscillates with small amplitude at a place where g = 10 m/s$^2$. What is its time period?","image":"","options":["3.14 s","1.57 s","6.28 s","0.99 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: L = 2.5 m, g = 10 m/s$^2$.\nFormula: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{L}{g}}$\nSubstituting: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{2.5}{10}} = 2\\pi \\times 0.5$\nSo T = $\\pi$ s ≈ 3.14 s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":35,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"The vibrations of a quartz crystal in a watch have a period of 2 µs. What is the frequency of these vibrations?","image":"","options":["$5 \\times 10^{5}$ Hz","$2 \\times 10^{6}$ Hz","$5 \\times 10^{-7}$ Hz","$5 \\times 10^{4}$ Hz"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $T = 2\\ \\mu s = 2 \\times 10^{-6}$ s\nFormula: $\\nu = \\frac{1}{T}$\nSubstituting: $\\nu = \\frac{1}{2 \\times 10^{-6}}$ Hz\nSo ν = $5 \\times 10^{5}$ Hz (500 kHz).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":36,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The function $x(t) = \\cos^2\\omega t$ is periodic with period $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$.\nReason (R): $\\cos^2\\omega t$ can be written as $\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2\\omega t$, which oscillates harmonically about $x = \\frac{1}{2}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"R is true: using $\\cos 2\\omega t = 2\\cos^2\\omega t - 1$ gives $\\cos^2\\omega t = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2\\omega t$. This is a harmonic variation about $\\frac{1}{2}$.\nA is false: the angular frequency of this variation is $2\\omega$, so the least period is $\\frac{2\\pi}{2\\omega} = \\frac{\\pi}{\\omega}$.\nThe function does repeat after $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$, but the period is the smallest repeating interval, which is $\\frac{\\pi}{\\omega}$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":37,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"The general result $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{I}{mgd}}$ holds for any rigid body swinging through small angles about a fixed horizontal axis. Here I is the moment of inertia about the axis and d is the distance of the centre of mass from the axis. A uniform rod of length 1.5 m is pivoted at one end and swings in a vertical plane. Its moment of inertia about the end is $\\frac{m\\ell^2}{3}$. Find its period. (g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["2.43 s","1.99 s","1.72 s","1.40 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\ell$ = 1.5 m, $I = \\frac{m\\ell^2}{3}$, $d = \\frac{\\ell}{2}$ = 0.75 m.\nThe restoring torque is $-mgd\\sin\\theta$, so $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{I}{mgd}}$.\nSubstituting: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{m\\ell^2/3}{mg\\ell/2}} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\ell}{3g}} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{0.1}$\nSo T ≈ 1.99 s. Treating the rod as a simple pendulum of length 1.5 m wrongly gives 2.43 s.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":38,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moves as $x = A\\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi t}{T}\\right)$. Where is the particle at $t = \\frac{3T}{4}$, and in which direction is it moving?","image":"","options":["At x = 0, moving towards +x","At x = 0, moving towards –x","At x = –A, momentarily at rest","At x = +A, momentarily at rest"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Phase at $t = 3T/4$ is $\\frac{2\\pi}{T}\\cdot\\frac{3T}{4} = \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$. \nPosition: $x = A\\cos(3\\pi/2) = 0$, so the particle is at the mean position. \nVelocity: $v = -A\\omega\\sin(3\\pi/2) = +A\\omega$, so it moves towards +x. \nThis matches the sequence +A → 0 → –A → 0 → +A at intervals of T/4. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":39,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle executing S.H.M. has a maximum speed of 30 cm/s and a maximum acceleration of 60 cm/s$^{2}$. The period of oscillation is","image":"","options":["$\\pi$ s.","$\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ s.","$2\\pi$ s.","$\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ s."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $v_{max} = 30$ cm/s and $a_{max} = 60$ cm/s$^2$.\nFormula: $v_{max} = A\\omega$ and $a_{max} = A\\omega^2$, so $\\omega = \\frac{a_{max}}{v_{max}}$.\nSubstituting: $\\omega = \\frac{60}{30} = 2$ rad/s.\nSo $T = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega} = \\frac{2\\pi}{2} = \\pi$ s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 14 (MCQ Q14.10)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":40,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle of total energy E executes SHM of amplitude A. Match List-I (position) with List-II (energy).\nList-I\n(a) x = 0\n(b) x = ±A\n(c) $x = \\pm\\frac{A}{\\sqrt{2}}$\n(d) $x = \\pm\\frac{A}{2}$\nList-II\n(i) K = U = E/2\n(ii) K = E, U = 0\n(iii) K = 0, U = E\n(iv) K = 3E/4","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Use $U = E\\frac{x^2}{A^2}$ and K = E − U.\n(a) At x = 0: U = 0, so K = E, which is (ii).\n(b) At x = ±A: U = E, so K = 0, which is (iii).\n(c) At $x = A/\\sqrt{2}$: U = E/2, so K = E/2, which is (i).\n(d) At x = A/2: U = E/4, so K = 3E/4, which is (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":41,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Any periodic function can be expressed as a superposition of sine and cosine functions of different time periods with suitable coefficients.\nStatement II: The function $A\\sin\\omega t + B\\cos\\omega t$ has the same period $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$ as $\\sin\\omega t$ alone.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. This is Fourier's theorem as stated in NCERT, and it is the reason sine and cosine functions are so important.\nStatement II: Correct. Both terms repeat after $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$, so their linear combination also has period $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":42,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"For a particle executing SHM:\nStatement I: The velocity–time graph and the displacement–time graph differ in phase by $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\nStatement II: The speed of the particle is maximum at the extreme positions, where the restoring force is also maximum.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. With $x = A\\cos\\omega t$, the velocity is $v = -\\omega A\\sin\\omega t = \\omega A\\cos(\\omega t + \\frac{\\pi}{2})$. This is a phase difference of $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\nStatement II is incorrect. The restoring force is indeed maximum at the extremes. But the speed there is zero; the speed is maximum ($\\omega A$) at the mean position.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":43,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle in SHM has a maximum speed of 0.6 m/s and a maximum acceleration of 1.8 m/s^2. What is its speed at the instant when the magnitude of its acceleration is 0.9 m/s^2?","image":"","options":["0.52 m/s","0.30 m/s","0.45 m/s","0.60 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\omega A = 0.6$ m/s and $\\omega^2 A = 1.8$ m/s^2\nDividing: $\\omega = \\frac{1.8}{0.6} = 3$ rad/s, so $A = \\frac{0.6}{3} = 0.2$ m\nFrom $|a| = \\omega^2 x$: $x = \\frac{0.9}{9} = 0.1$ m\nFormula: $v = \\omega\\sqrt{A^2 - x^2} = 3\\sqrt{0.04 - 0.01}$\nSo v ≈ 0.52 m/s\nHalving $v_{max}$ to get 0.30 m/s is wrong, because v does not vary linearly with x.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":44,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle executes SHM with amplitude 4 cm. At what distance from the mean position are its kinetic and potential energies equal?","image":"","options":["2.83 cm","2.00 cm","1.41 cm","3.46 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: A = 4 cm, and K = U.\nFormula: If K = U, then U = E/2, so $\\frac{1}{2}kx^2 = \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}kA^2$. This gives $x = \\frac{A}{\\sqrt{2}}$.\nSubstituting: $x = \\frac{4}{1.414}$\nSo x ≈ 2.83 cm. The value 2 cm (A/2) is where U = E/4 only.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":45,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle executes linear SHM between two points P and Q, 12 cm apart. The direction from P to Q is taken as positive. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) At 1 cm from Q, moving towards P: velocity is negative and acceleration is negative.\n(ii) At 4 cm from P, moving towards Q: velocity is positive and acceleration is negative.\n(iii) At the mid-point, moving towards Q: velocity is positive and acceleration is zero.\n(iv) At the end P: velocity is zero and force is positive.\n(v) At 5 cm from Q, moving towards P: velocity is positive and force is negative.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Take the mid-point as x = 0, so P is at −6 cm and Q is at +6 cm. Acceleration and force always have the sign opposite to x.\n(i) x = +5 cm, moving towards P, so v < 0 and a < 0. Correct.\n(ii) x = −2 cm, so a > 0. Incorrect.\n(iii) x = 0, so a = 0, and v > 0. Correct.\n(iv) x = −6 cm, so v = 0 and F > 0. Correct.\n(v) The particle moves towards P, so v < 0. Incorrect.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":46,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a particle in SHM passes through its mean position, its acceleration is zero even though its speed is maximum.\nReason (R): In SHM, the acceleration is proportional to the displacement from the mean position and is directed opposite to it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. At $x = 0$, $v = \\omega A$, which is maximum, while $a = -\\omega^2 \\times 0 = 0$.\nR is true. NCERT's Eq. (13.11) gives $a = -\\omega^2 x$.\nR explains A: acceleration depends on displacement, not on speed. At zero displacement, the acceleration is zero whatever the speed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":47,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"An SHM is given by $x = -4\\sin\\left(2t + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)$, with x in cm and t in s. You represent it with a reference circle on which the particle moves anticlockwise, with x as the projection on the x-axis. Which description of the reference circle is correct?","image":"","options":["Radius 4 cm, angular speed 2 rad/s, initial position at $120^\\circ$ from the +x axis","Radius 4 cm, angular speed 2 rad/s, initial position at $240^\\circ$ from the +x axis","Radius 4 cm, angular speed 2 rad/s, initial position at $30^\\circ$ from the +x axis","Radius 4 cm, angular speed 2 rad/s, initial position at $210^\\circ$ from the +x axis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Convert to cosine form: $-\\sin\\theta = \\cos(\\theta + \\pi/2)$.\nSo $x = 4\\cos\\left(2t + \\frac{\\pi}{6} + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 4\\cos\\left(2t + \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)$.\nThis gives radius 4 cm, angular speed 2 rad/s and initial angle $120^\\circ$.\nCheck: $x(0) = -2$ cm. Both $120^\\circ$ and $240^\\circ$ give x = −2 cm, but the velocity decides between them. From the equation, $v(0) = -8\\cos30^\\circ < 0$. Anticlockwise motion at $120^\\circ$ has a negative x-velocity, while at $240^\\circ$ it is positive.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":48,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"For a particle in SHM described by $x(t) = A\\cos(\\omega t + \\phi)$, the quantity $\\phi$ (the phase constant) is best described as:","image":"","options":["the value of the phase of the motion at t = 0","the maximum displacement of the particle from the mean position","the phase of the motion after one full period T","the angle swept by the reference particle per second"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The time-dependent argument $(\\omega t + \\phi)$ is called the phase of the motion. \nAt t = 0 the phase reduces to $\\phi$, so $\\phi$ is called the phase constant (phase angle). \nThe maximum displacement is the amplitude A. \nThe angle swept per second is $\\omega$. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":49,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Although the projection of uniform circular motion on a diameter is SHM, the force acting on a particle in linear SHM is very different from the centripetal force on the reference particle.\nReason (R): The centripetal force has constant magnitude and always points towards the centre of the circle. The restoring force in linear SHM acts along a line, varies in proportion to the displacement and is zero at the mean position.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that, despite the kinematic connection, the force in linear SHM is very different from the centripetal force.\nR is true. The centripetal force $m\\omega^2 A$ is constant in magnitude and rotates with the particle. The SHM force $F = -kx$ acts along one line and changes with x, becoming zero at x = 0.\nThis difference in nature, magnitude and direction is exactly why the two forces differ, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":50,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions of time represent periodic motion ($\\omega$ is a positive constant)?\n(i) $\\sin^2\\omega t$\n(ii) $e^{-\\omega t}\\sin\\omega t$\n(iii) $\\cos\\omega t + \\cos 3\\omega t$\n(iv) $\\log(\\omega t)$\n(v) $\\omega t + \\sin\\omega t$\n(vi) $e^{\\sin\\omega t}$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Periodic: $\\sin^2\\omega t = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2\\omega t$, with period $\\pi/\\omega$.\n(ii) Not periodic: the factor $e^{-\\omega t}$ makes the oscillation die out, so values never repeat.\n(iii) Periodic: $3\\omega$ is an integral multiple of $\\omega$, so the period is $2\\pi/\\omega$.\n(iv) Not periodic: $\\log(\\omega t)$ increases monotonically.\n(v) Not periodic: the $\\omega t$ term grows without bound.\n(vi) Periodic: $e^{\\sin\\omega t}$ repeats whenever $\\sin\\omega t$ repeats, with period $2\\pi/\\omega$.\nSo three functions are periodic: (i), (iii) and (vi).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":51,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"For a simple pendulum (bob of mass m, massless string of length L, angular displacement $\\theta$), match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) $T - mg\\cos\\theta$\n(B) $mg\\sin\\theta$\n(C) Moment of inertia about the support\n(D) $-mgL\\sin\\theta$\nList-II\n(I) $mL^2$\n(II) Restoring torque about the support\n(III) Provides the centripetal (radial) acceleration\n(IV) Provides the tangential acceleration","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The net radial force $T - mg\\cos\\theta$ supplies the radial acceleration $\\omega^2 L$, so A-III.\n(B) $mg\\sin\\theta$ acts along the arc and gives the tangential acceleration, so B-IV.\n(C) With a massless string, $I = mL^2$, so C-I.\n(D) $\\tau = -L(mg\\sin\\theta)$ is the restoring torque about the support, so D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":52,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A simple pendulum of length 0.50 m hangs inside a car. The car moves with uniform speed 20 m/s on a horizontal circular track of radius 40 m. What is the period of small oscillations of the pendulum about its equilibrium position? (Take g = 10 m/s$^2$.)","image":"","options":["1.18 s","1.40 s","0.99 s","1.67 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: L = 0.50 m, v = 20 m/s, R = 40 m, g = 10 m/s$^2$.\nThe centripetal acceleration is $a = \\frac{v^2}{R} = \\frac{400}{40}$ = 10 m/s$^2$ (horizontal).\nThis is perpendicular to g, so $g_{eff} = \\sqrt{g^2 + a^2} = \\sqrt{200}$ ≈ 14.14 m/s$^2$.\nFormula: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{L}{g_{eff}}} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{0.50}{14.14}}$.\nSo T ≈ 1.18 s.\nAdding the accelerations directly (g + a = 20 m/s$^2$) wrongly gives 0.99 s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":53,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"The piston of an engine has a stroke (twice the amplitude) of 0.8 m and moves with simple harmonic motion at an angular frequency of 300 rad/min. What is its maximum speed?","image":"","options":["4.0 m/s","1.0 m/s","2.0 m/s","0.4 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: stroke = 0.8 m, so amplitude A = 0.4 m. ω = 300 rad/min = 300/60 = 5 rad/s.\nFormula: $v_{max} = \\omega A$\nSubstituting: $v_{max} = 5 \\times 0.4$\nSo maximum speed = 2.0 m/s. Using the full stroke as the amplitude gives the wrong value of 4.0 m/s.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":54,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A simple pendulum makes an angle $\\theta$ with the vertical. Its bob is acted on by the tension T in the string and its weight mg. Which force provides the restoring torque about the point of support?","image":"","options":["The tension T along the string","The net radial force $T - mg\\cos\\theta$","The tangential component $mg\\sin\\theta$","The component $mg\\cos\\theta$ along the string"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Resolve mg into two parts: $mg\\cos\\theta$ along the string and $mg\\sin\\theta$ perpendicular to it.\nT and $mg\\cos\\theta$ both act along the string, which passes through the support. So their torque about the support is zero.\nThe radial force $T - mg\\cos\\theta$ only supplies the centripetal acceleration.\nOnly $mg\\sin\\theta$ has a lever arm L, giving the restoring torque $\\tau = -mgL\\sin\\theta$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":55,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle P moves uniformly on a circle of radius 0.2 m with angular speed 5 rad/s. Its projection P′ on a diameter of the circle executes SHM. The amplitude and angular frequency of this SHM are:","image":"","options":["0.2 m and 5 rad/s","0.4 m and 5 rad/s","0.2 m and 2.5 rad/s","0.1 m and 10 rad/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The projection of P on a diameter is $x(t) = A\\cos(\\omega t + \\phi)$, where A is the radius of the reference circle and $\\omega$ is the angular speed of the reference particle.\nSo the amplitude is the radius, 0.2 m, not the diameter 0.4 m.\nThe angular frequency is the angular speed, 5 rad/s. It is not halved or doubled.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":56,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A ball tied to a string is whirled in a horizontal circle with constant angular speed. A lamp casts the ball's shadow on a vertical wall that is perpendicular to the plane of the circle. The shadow on the wall:","image":"","options":["moves uniformly in a circle of the same radius","moves to and fro along a horizontal line, executing SHM about the point corresponding to the centre of rotation","moves to and fro along a vertical line with constant speed","remains stationary because the ball's speed is constant"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The shadow shows only the component of the ball's motion along the diameter that is normal to the viewing direction.\nThis component is the projection of uniform circular motion on a diameter, which is SHM.\nThe shadow therefore oscillates along a horizontal line, with the point of rotation as its midpoint.\nIts speed is not constant: it is maximum at the centre and zero at the ends. So option (3) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":57,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"In the generalised sense used for oscillations, a displacement variable is any physical quantity that changes with time about a mean value. How many of the following can serve as a displacement variable?\n(i) Angle made by a simple pendulum's string with the vertical\n(ii) Voltage across a capacitor in an AC circuit\n(iii) Pressure variation at a point as a sound wave passes\n(iv) Electric field at a point in a light wave","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT treats the angular displacement θ of a pendulum as a displacement variable.\n(ii) Correct: the time-varying capacitor voltage in an AC circuit is a displacement variable.\n(iii) Correct: pressure variations in a sound wave are a displacement variable.\n(iv) Correct: the changing electric and magnetic fields in a light wave are also displacement variables.\nAll four qualify.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":58,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Two pendulums have periods of 1.2 s and 1.5 s. At t = 0 both are released together from their right-hand extreme positions. After what minimum time will both again be at their right-hand extreme positions at the same instant?","image":"","options":["6.0 s","2.7 s","0.3 s","1.8 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $T_1 = 1.2$ s and $T_2 = 1.5$ s.\nEach motion repeats only after integer multiples of its own period, $n_1 T_1$ and $n_2 T_2$.\nWe need the least common time: $n_1 (1.2) = n_2 (1.5)$, i.e., $4n_1 = 5n_2$. The smallest solution is $n_1 = 5$, $n_2 = 4$.\nSo t = 5 × 1.2 = 6.0 s, the LCM of the two periods.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":59,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"What is the period of the function $f(t) = \\cos 2\\omega t + \\sin 3\\omega t$?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{\\omega}$","$\\frac{2\\pi}{3\\omega}$","$\\frac{6\\pi}{\\omega}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Period of $\\cos 2\\omega t$ = $\\frac{\\pi}{\\omega}$.\nPeriod of $\\sin 3\\omega t$ = $\\frac{2\\pi}{3\\omega}$.\nThe sum repeats only when both terms repeat together, so we need the smallest common multiple.\nMultiples of $\\frac{\\pi}{\\omega}$: $\\frac{\\pi}{\\omega}, \\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}, ...$\nMultiples of $\\frac{2\\pi}{3\\omega}$: $\\frac{2\\pi}{3\\omega}, \\frac{4\\pi}{3\\omega}, \\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}, ...$\nThe least common value is $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":60,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"For a particle of mass m in SHM with amplitude A, angular frequency $\\omega$ and period T, match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Phase difference between acceleration and displacement\n(B) Period of potential energy\n(C) Velocity amplitude\n(D) Force constant k\nList-II\n(i) $\\omega A$\n(ii) $m\\omega^2$\n(iii) $\\pi$\n(iv) $T/2$","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii","A-iv, B-iii, C-i, D-ii","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-i, B-iv, C-iii, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $a = -\\omega^2 x$, so acceleration is opposite to displacement. The phase difference is $\\pi$, giving (iii).\n(B) $U = \\frac{1}{2}kA^2\\cos^2(\\omega t+\\phi)$ repeats after $T/2$, giving (iv).\n(C) $v = -\\omega A\\sin(\\omega t+\\phi)$, so the maximum speed is $\\omega A$, giving (i).\n(D) From $F = ma = -m\\omega^2 x = -kx$, we get $k = m\\omega^2$, giving (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":61,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Two x–t graphs are drawn for SHM using $x = A\\cos(\\omega t + \\phi)$ with $\\phi = 0$. Both curves start at x = +A at t = 0 and have the same maximum height. In the time curve (a) completes one full cycle, curve (b) completes two full cycles. Which statement is correct?","image":"","options":["Curve (b) has the larger amplitude","Curve (b) has a different phase constant from curve (a)","Curve (b) has twice the angular frequency and half the period of curve (a)","Curve (b) has half the angular frequency of curve (a)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Both curves have the same maximum height, so the amplitudes are equal. \nBoth start at +A, so both have $\\phi = 0$. \nCurve (b) repeats twice as often, so its period is $T/2$. \nSince $\\omega = 2\\pi/T$, halving T doubles $\\omega$. \nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":62,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"For SHM described by $x(t) = A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)$, match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) $A$\n(B) $\\omega t+\\phi$\n(C) $\\phi$\n(D) $\\omega$\nList-II:\n(i) Value of the phase at $t = 0$\n(ii) $\\frac{2\\pi}{T}$\n(iii) Magnitude of maximum displacement\n(iv) Time-dependent argument of the cosine","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii","A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii","A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A) The amplitude A is the magnitude of the maximum displacement → (iii).\n(B) The phase $\\omega t+\\phi$ is the time-dependent argument of the cosine → (iv).\n(C) The phase constant $\\phi$ is the value of the phase at $t=0$ → (i).\n(D) The angular frequency is $\\omega = \\frac{2\\pi}{T}$ → (ii).\nOption (1) wrongly swaps the phase and the phase constant.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":63,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following relations between acceleration $a$ and displacement $x$ does the particle execute simple harmonic motion?","image":"","options":["$a = 16x$","$a = -16x$","$a = -16x^2$","$a = 16x^3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Condition: SHM requires $a = -\\omega^2 x$. The acceleration must be linear in x and directed opposite to the displacement.\n$a = -16x$ satisfies this, with $\\omega$ = 4 rad/s.\n$a = 16x$ is linear in x, but it points away from the mean position, so it is not restoring.\n$a = -16x^2$ and $a = 16x^3$ are not proportional to x.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":64,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A block attached to a light spring of spring constant $200\\ \\text{N m}^{-1}$ oscillates on a frictionless horizontal surface with an amplitude of 5 cm. What is the total mechanical energy of the oscillator?","image":"","options":["0.125 J","0.25 J","0.50 J","2.5 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $k = 200\\ \\text{N m}^{-1}$, $A = 5\\ \\text{cm} = 0.05$ m.\nFormula: $E = \\frac{1}{2}kA^2$. This is the potential energy at an extreme position, where the kinetic energy is zero.\nSubstituting: $E = \\frac{1}{2}\\times 200 \\times (0.05)^2 = 100 \\times 0.0025$.\nSo E = 0.25 J. Option (3), 0.50 J, comes from forgetting the factor $\\frac{1}{2}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":65,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"The displacement of a particle varies with time as $x(t) = \\sin\\left(\\frac{2\\pi t}{3}\\right) + \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right)$, where t is in seconds. What is the frequency of this periodic motion?","image":"","options":["0.083 Hz","0.58 Hz","0.25 Hz","0.14 Hz"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"First term: $\\omega_1 = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}$, so $T_1 = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega_1} = 3$ s.\nSecond term: $\\omega_2 = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, so $T_2 = 4$ s.\nThe sum repeats only when both terms repeat. The least such time is LCM(3 s, 4 s) = 12 s, so T = 12 s.\n$\\nu = \\frac{1}{T} = \\frac{1}{12}$ Hz ≈ 0.083 Hz.\nOption (2) wrongly adds the frequencies (1/3 + 1/4 ≈ 0.58 Hz).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":66,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Two particles execute SHM along parallel lines with the same angular frequency:\n$x_1 = 4\\sin(\\omega t + \\pi/6)$ and $x_2 = 3\\cos\\omega t$.\nWhich statement about their phase relationship is correct?","image":"","options":["$x_2$ leads $x_1$ by $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$","$x_1$ leads $x_2$ by $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$","$x_2$ leads $x_1$ by $\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$","$x_1$ leads $x_2$ by $\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Write both in cosine form. \n$x_1 = 4\\cos(\\omega t + \\pi/6 - \\pi/2) = 4\\cos(\\omega t - \\pi/3)$. \n$x_2 = 3\\cos(\\omega t + 0)$. \nPhase of $x_2$ minus phase of $x_1$ = $0 - (-\\pi/3) = +\\pi/3$. \nSo $x_2$ leads by $\\pi/3$; the different amplitudes do not affect phase. \nTaking $\\pi/6$ as $x_1$'s cosine phase leads to the wrong answers. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":67,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A simple pendulum of length 0.40 m is taken to the surface of the Moon, where the acceleration due to gravity is 1.6 m/s$^2$. What is its period for small oscillations there?","image":"","options":["3.14 s","1.26 s","6.28 s","0.50 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: L = 0.40 m, $g_{moon}$ = 1.6 m/s$^2$.\nFormula: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{L}{g}}$.\nSubstituting: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{0.40}{1.6}} = 2\\pi \\times 0.5$.\nSo T = $\\pi$ ≈ 3.14 s.\nThe option 1.26 s comes from wrongly using Earth's g = 10 m/s$^2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":68,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions of time represent simple harmonic motion ($\\omega$ is a positive constant)?\n(i) $2\\sin\\omega t - 5\\cos\\omega t$\n(ii) $\\sin\\omega t + \\sin 2\\omega t$\n(iii) $\\cos(2\\omega t + \\pi/3)$\n(iv) $e^{-\\omega t}\\sin\\omega t$\n(v) $5\\sin(\\pi/3 - \\omega t)$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) SHM: a sine and a cosine of the same $\\omega$ combine into a single sinusoid of amplitude $\\sqrt{29}$. \n(ii) Not SHM: two different frequencies give a periodic motion (period $2\\pi/\\omega$) that is not a single sinusoid. \n(iii) SHM: a single sinusoid with angular frequency $2\\omega$. \n(iv) Non-periodic: the amplitude keeps decaying. \n(v) SHM: it equals $-5\\sin(\\omega t - \\pi/3)$, a single sinusoid. \nSo 3 functions represent SHM. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":69,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In SHM, the magnitude of acceleration of the particle is maximum at the extreme positions and zero at the mean position.\nReason (R): In SHM, acceleration is proportional to displacement from the mean position and is always directed towards the mean position.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: $a = -\\omega^2 x$, so acceleration is proportional to x and directed towards the mean position.\nA is true: since $|a| = \\omega^2|x|$, it is zero at $x = 0$ and maximum, equal to $\\omega^2 A$, at $x = \\pm A$.\nA follows directly from R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":70,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle executing SHM of period 2.4 s passes through its mean position at t = 0. What is the earliest time after t = 0 at which its potential energy becomes three times its kinetic energy?","image":"","options":["0.4 s","0.3 s","0.6 s","0.2 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: T = 2.4 s; the particle starts from the mean position, so $x = A\\sin\\omega t$.\nCondition: U = 3K means U = 3E/4, so $x^2 = \\frac{3}{4}A^2$ and $x = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}A$.\nSo $\\sin\\omega t = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, giving $\\omega t = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$ and $t = \\frac{T}{6}$.\nSubstituting: t = 2.4/6 = 0.4 s. The value 0.3 s (T/8) is when K = U.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":71,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A U-tube of uniform cross-section contains a mercury column of total length 40 cm. One level is depressed slightly and released. Neglecting viscosity, find the period of oscillation of the column. (Take g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["1.257 s","0.889 s","0.628 s","0.444 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: L = 0.4 m, g = 10 m/s$^2$.\nIf one level drops by y, the other rises by y. The level difference 2y gives a restoring force $F = -2\\rho g A y$ on a mass $\\rho A L$.\nFormula: $\\omega^2 = \\frac{2g}{L}$, so $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{L}{2g}}$.\nSubstituting: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{0.4}{20}} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{0.02}$\nSo T ≈ 0.889 s.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":72,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"For a linear harmonic oscillator of amplitude A and total energy E, consider the following statements:\n(i) The graph of kinetic energy versus displacement is a downward-opening parabola with its maximum at $x = 0$.\n(ii) Both kinetic energy and potential energy reach their peak values twice in each period.\n(iii) At $x = A/2$, the kinetic energy equals $\\frac{3}{4}E$.\n(iv) The total energy is directly proportional to the amplitude.\n(v) The potential energy would be positive no matter how the arbitrary constant in its definition is chosen.\nHow many of these statements are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $K = \\frac{1}{2}k(A^2 - x^2)$, an inverted parabola that is maximum at $x = 0$.\n(ii) Correct: each energy has period $T/2$, so it peaks twice per cycle.\n(iii) Correct: $U = \\frac{1}{2}k\\frac{A^2}{4} = \\frac{E}{4}$, so $K = \\frac{3E}{4}$.\n(iv) Incorrect: $E \\propto A^2$, not A.\n(v) Incorrect: NCERT says U is positive only by our choice of the undetermined constant.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":73,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a block oscillating on a frictionless surface under a spring force, the total mechanical energy stays constant even though the kinetic and potential energies keep changing with time.\nReason (R): The spring force $F = -kx$ is a conservative force with an associated potential energy $\\frac{1}{2}kx^2$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: $K + U = \\frac{1}{2}kA^2[\\sin^2(\\omega t+\\phi)+\\cos^2(\\omega t+\\phi)] = \\frac{1}{2}kA^2$, which does not depend on time.\nR is true: the spring force is conservative, so a potential energy $U = \\frac{1}{2}kx^2$ can be defined for it.\nR explains A: mechanical energy is conserved whenever only conservative forces do work, as NCERT points out.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":74,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following relations (x = displacement, a = acceleration, F = force, all constants positive) describe simple harmonic motion?\n(i) $a = -9x$\n(ii) $F = -4x$\n(iii) $a = 5x$\n(iv) $a = -3(x - 2)$\n(v) $F = -2x^3$\n(vi) $a = -x^2$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"SHM requires the restoring force or acceleration to be proportional to displacement from a mean position and opposite to it.\n(i) Yes: $\\omega$ = 3 rad/s.\n(ii) Yes: Hooke-type force law.\n(iii) No: the force pushes the particle away from the mean position.\n(iv) Yes: SHM about x = 2, since $a = -3X$ with X = x − 2.\n(v) No: non-linear in x.\n(vi) No: non-linear and not always restoring.\nSo three relations describe SHM.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":75,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A 2 kg block attached to a spring of spring constant 200 N/m oscillates on a frictionless horizontal surface with an amplitude of 10 cm. What is the speed of the block when it is 6 cm from the mean position?","image":"","options":["0.8 m/s","1.0 m/s","0.6 m/s","0.4 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 2 kg, k = 200 N/m, A = 0.10 m, x = 0.06 m.\nFormula: $\\omega = \\sqrt{k/m}$. Energy conservation, $\\frac{1}{2}kA^2 = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2 + \\frac{1}{2}kx^2$, gives $v = \\omega\\sqrt{A^2 - x^2}$.\nSubstituting: $\\omega = \\sqrt{200/2} = 10$ rad/s, and $v = 10\\sqrt{0.01 - 0.0036} = 10 \\times 0.08$.\nSo v = 0.8 m/s.\nThe option 1.0 m/s is the maximum speed $\\omega A$, which occurs only at the mean position.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":76,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"For a simple pendulum of length $L$ with a bob of mass $m$, oscillating with small amplitude, which statement is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Torque about the support is provided entirely by the tangential component $mg\\sin\\theta$","The tension in the string equals $mg$ when the bob passes through the mean position","The net radial force $T - mg\\cos\\theta$ supplies the centripetal force","The time period does not depend on the mass of the bob"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the radial forces pass through the support, so they produce no torque. Hence $\\tau = -L\\,mg\\sin\\theta$.\n(2) Incorrect: at the mean position the bob moves on a circle with speed v. So $T - mg = \\frac{mv^2}{L}$, which gives $T > mg$.\n(3) Correct: the net radial force $T - mg\\cos\\theta$ provides the radial acceleration.\n(4) Correct: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{L/g}$ contains no m, because I = $mL^2$ and the m cancels.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":77,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The kinetic energy of a particle executing SHM of period T is itself periodic, with period $\\frac{T}{2}$.\nReason (R): Kinetic energy depends on the square of the velocity, so the direction (sign) of velocity does not affect it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: $K = \\frac{1}{2}kA^2\\sin^2(\\omega t+\\phi)$, and $\\sin^2$ repeats every $\\frac{T}{2}$.\nR is true: $K = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$, so $+v$ and $-v$ give the same K.\nThe speed takes the same set of values in each half-cycle. Velocity has opposite signs in the two halves, but K ignores the sign. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":78,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A 2 kg block on a spring of constant 800 N/m performs SHM described by $x(t) = A\\cos(\\omega t + \\phi)$ with total energy 1 J. At t = 0, its kinetic energy is 0.75 J, its displacement is positive, and it is moving in the +x direction. What is the phase constant $\\phi$?","image":"","options":["$-\\frac{\\pi}{3}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{3}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{6}$","$-\\frac{\\pi}{6}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: E = 1 J, so U(0) = 1 − 0.75 = 0.25 J.\nAmplitude: $A = \\sqrt{2E/k} = \\sqrt{2/800} = 0.05$ m. Initial position: $x_0 = \\sqrt{2(0.25)/800} = 0.025$ m.\nUsing $x_0 = A\\cos\\phi$: $\\cos\\phi = 0.5$, so $\\phi = \\pm\\frac{\\pi}{3}$.\nVelocity: $v(0) = -\\omega A\\sin\\phi > 0$ requires $\\sin\\phi < 0$, so $\\phi = -\\frac{\\pi}{3}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":79,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle P moves uniformly on a reference circle of radius A, and P′ is its projection on the x-axis. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) P′ has its maximum speed when P lies on the y-axis.\n(ii) The acceleration of P′ is the x-component of the centripetal acceleration of P, equal to $-\\omega^2 x$.\n(iii) The projection of P on the y-axis performs SHM of amplitude $A/\\sqrt{2}$.\n(iv) If P moves clockwise instead of anticlockwise, its x-projection no longer performs SHM.\n(v) The period of P′ equals the period of revolution of P.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: when P is on the y-axis, its velocity is entirely along x, and P′ is at the centre.\n(ii) Correct: projecting $\\omega^2 A$ directed along PO gives $-\\omega^2 A\\cos(\\omega t + \\phi) = -\\omega^2 x$.\n(iii) Incorrect: the y-projection has the same amplitude A.\n(iv) Incorrect: clockwise motion only changes the sign of $\\omega t$ in the phase, and the projection is still SHM.\n(v) Correct: P′ repeats its motion once per revolution of P.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":80,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) The period of SHM does not depend on its amplitude or energy.\n(b) The sum of any two SHMs along the same line, whatever their frequencies, is always periodic.\n(c) $\\sin\\omega t + \\cos 3\\omega t$ is periodic with period $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$.\n(d) $\\sin^2\\omega t$ represents a harmonic motion about the point $\\frac{1}{2}$, with period $\\frac{\\pi}{\\omega}$.\n(e) For a linear SHM of given $\\omega$, two initial conditions are necessary and sufficient to fix the motion completely.\n(f) Damped SHM is strictly simple harmonic for all times.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{m/k}$ involves neither A nor E.\n(b) Incorrect: the sum is periodic only if one frequency is an integral multiple of the other.\n(c) Correct: the two terms have periods $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$ and $\\frac{2\\pi}{3\\omega}$, so the sum repeats after $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$.\n(d) Correct: $\\sin^2\\omega t = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2\\omega t$.\n(e) Correct: for example, initial position and initial velocity.\n(f) Incorrect: damped SHM is approximately simple harmonic only for times much less than 2m/b.\nCorrect statements: a, c, d, e, which makes 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":81,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions of time represent simple harmonic motion? ($\\omega$ is a positive constant; A and B are constants, not both zero)\n(i) $\\sin\\omega t + \\cos\\omega t$\n(ii) $2\\sin\\omega t\\cos\\omega t$\n(iii) $\\sin\\omega t + \\cos 2\\omega t$\n(iv) $e^{-\\omega t}$\n(v) $A\\cos\\omega t + B\\sin\\omega t$\n(vi) $\\log(\\omega t)$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) SHM: it equals $\\sqrt{2}\\sin(\\omega t + \\pi/4)$, a single sinusoid.\n(ii) SHM: it equals $\\sin 2\\omega t$, with angular frequency $2\\omega$.\n(iii) Not SHM: it is periodic with period $2\\pi/\\omega$, but it is the sum of two different frequencies.\n(iv) Not SHM: it decreases monotonically and is non-periodic.\n(v) SHM: it can be written as $D\\sin(\\omega t+\\phi)$.\n(vi) Not SHM: it increases monotonically and is non-periodic.\nSo three functions, (i), (ii) and (v), represent SHM.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":82,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle is acted simultaneously by mutually perpendicular simple harmonic motions $x = a\\cos\\omega t$ and $y = a\\sin\\omega t$. The trajectory of motion of the particle will be","image":"","options":["an ellipse.","a parabola.","a circle.","a straight line."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $x = a\\cos\\omega t$ and $y = a\\sin\\omega t$. The two motions have equal amplitudes and a phase difference of $\\pi/2$.\nSquare and add: $x^2 + y^2 = a^2(\\cos^2\\omega t + \\sin^2\\omega t) = a^2$.\nThis is a circle of radius $a$ centred at the origin.\nAn ellipse (option 1) would need unequal amplitudes. A straight line (option 4) would need a phase difference of $0$ or $\\pi$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 14 (MCQ Q14.5)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":83,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Match the frequency in List-I with the corresponding period in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) 50 Hz\n(B) 2.5 Hz\n(C) 1 MHz\n(D) 0.25 Hz\nList-II\n(I) 4 s\n(II) 1 µs\n(III) 0.4 s\n(IV) 20 ms","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Use $T = \\frac{1}{\\nu}$ for each pair.\n(A) $\\frac{1}{50}$ s = 0.02 s = 20 ms, so A matches IV.\n(B) $\\frac{1}{2.5}$ s = 0.4 s, so B matches III.\n(C) $\\frac{1}{10^{6}}$ s = 1 µs, so C matches II.\n(D) $\\frac{1}{0.25}$ s = 4 s, so D matches I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":84,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following motions is periodic but NOT oscillatory?","image":"","options":["A ball rolling to and fro near the bottom of a smooth bowl","The bob of a wall-clock pendulum","A ball tied to a string and whirled in a horizontal circle with constant angular speed","A boat tossing up and down on a river"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Oscillatory motion is a to and fro motion about a mean (equilibrium) position that lies within the path.\nUniform circular motion repeats after equal intervals, so it is periodic. However, the body never moves back and forth about an equilibrium point, so it is not oscillatory.\nThe ball in a bowl, the clock pendulum and the tossing boat all move to and fro about a mean position. They are both periodic and oscillatory.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":85,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A block of mass 2 kg on a frictionless horizontal surface is connected between two identical springs, each of spring constant 100 N/m, whose other ends are fixed to rigid walls on either side. The block is displaced by 5 cm from equilibrium and released. What is the total mechanical energy of the oscillation?","image":"","options":["0.25 J","0.125 J","0.5 J","0.0625 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: k = 100 N/m for each spring, A = 0.05 m.\nWhen the block is displaced by x, one spring stretches and the other compresses by x. Both springs push the block towards the mean position, so $F = -2kx$ and the effective constant is $2k = 200$ N/m.\nFormula: $E = \\frac{1}{2}k_{eff}A^2$.\nSubstituting: $E = \\frac{1}{2}(200)(0.05)^2 = 0.25$ J.\nUsing a single k would give 0.125 J, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":86,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: $x = 3\\sin\\omega t + 4\\cos\\omega t$ represents a simple harmonic motion of amplitude 5 units.\nStatement II: $x = \\cos^2\\omega t$ represents a simple harmonic motion about x = 0 with period $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. $A\\sin\\omega t + B\\cos\\omega t = D\\sin(\\omega t + \\phi)$ with $D = \\sqrt{A^2+B^2} = \\sqrt{9+16} = 5$. \nStatement II is incorrect. $\\cos^2\\omega t = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2\\omega t$. \nIts oscillation is centred at $\\frac{1}{2}$, not at 0. \nIts period is $\\frac{\\pi}{\\omega}$, not $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$. \nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":87,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A heart beating 75 times per minute has a beat frequency of 1.25 Hz.\nReason (R): One hertz is defined as one oscillation per minute.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A: 75 beats in 60 s gives 75/60 = 1.25 $s^{-1}$ = 1.25 Hz. A is true.\nR: 1 Hz = 1 oscillation per second, not per minute. R is false.\nIf R were true, the frequency would be 75 Hz, not 1.25 Hz.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":88,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the restoring force on a particle executing linear SHM is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["It is zero when the particle passes through the mean position.","Its magnitude is maximum at the extreme positions.","It is always directed away from the mean position.","Its magnitude is directly proportional to the displacement from the mean position."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Use the force law $F = -kx$ to judge each statement.\n(1) Correct: x = 0 gives F = 0.\n(2) Correct: |x| is largest (= A) at the extremes, so |F| is largest there.\n(3) Incorrect: the negative sign means F always points towards the mean position. That is why it is called a restoring force.\n(4) Correct: |F| = k|x|.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":89,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Consider a particle executing linear SHM between $x = -A$ and $x = +A$.\nStatement I: The velocity of the particle is zero at both extreme positions.\nStatement II: The acceleration of the particle is zero at both extreme positions.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. From $v = \\omega\\sqrt{A^2 - x^2}$, the speed is zero when $x = \\pm A$.\nStatement II is incorrect. Since $a = -\\omega^2 x$, the acceleration has its maximum magnitude $\\omega^2 A$ at the extremes. It is zero only at the mean position.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":90,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about a simple pendulum oscillating with small amplitude are correct?\n(i) Its period does not depend on the mass of the bob.\n(ii) Doubling the length of the string doubles the period.\n(iii) A seconds pendulum has a period of 1 s.\n(iv) The approximation $\\sin\\theta \\approx \\theta$ holds only when $\\theta$ is expressed in radians.\n(v) At an amplitude of 20°, $\\sin\\theta$ differs greatly from $\\theta$, so the motion cannot be treated as nearly simple harmonic.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: m cancels out, giving $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{L}{g}}$.\n(ii) Incorrect: $T \\propto \\sqrt{L}$, so the period becomes $\\sqrt{2}$ times.\n(iii) Incorrect: a seconds pendulum ticks each second, so its period is 2 s.\n(iv) Correct: the series $\\sin\\theta = \\theta - \\frac{\\theta^3}{3!} + ...$ is valid with $\\theta$ in radians.\n(v) Incorrect: $\\sin 20° = 0.342$ while $\\theta$ = 0.349 rad, which are nearly equal.\nTwo statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":91,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle revolves clockwise with uniform speed on a circle of radius 4 cm centred at the origin, with a period of 8 s. At $t = 0$, its radius vector makes an angle of $60^\\circ$ with the positive x-axis, measured anticlockwise (the particle is in the first quadrant). What is the x-projection of its radius vector at $t = 2$ s?","image":"","options":["3.46 cm","-3.46 cm","2.0 cm","-2.0 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: A = 4 cm, T = 8 s, initial angle $\\pi/3$, sense of rotation clockwise.\nFormula: in clockwise rotation the angle decreases with time, so it equals $\\pi/3 - \\frac{2\\pi t}{T}$. Hence $x = A\\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi t}{T} - \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$.\nSubstituting t = 2 s: the phase is $\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$, so $x = 4\\cos(\\pi/6)$.\nSo x = 4 × 0.866 = 3.46 cm. Taking the rotation as anticlockwise by mistake gives -3.46 cm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":92,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"The relation between acceleration and displacement of four particles are given below. Which one of the particles is executing simple harmonic motion?","image":"","options":["$a_x = +2x$","$a_x = +2x^2$","$a_x = -2x^2$","$a_x = -2x$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Condition for SHM: $a = -\\omega^2 x$. The acceleration must be proportional to $x$ (linear) and directed opposite to the displacement.\n(1) $+2x$: linear, but the sign is wrong, so it is not a restoring force.\n(2) and (3): these depend on $x^2$, so they are not linear.\n(4) $-2x$: linear and restoring, so it is SHM with $\\omega = \\sqrt{2}$ rad/s.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 14 (MCQ Q14.3)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":93,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: At a place where g = 9.8 m/s$^2$, the length of a seconds pendulum is very nearly 1 m.\nStatement II: A seconds pendulum completes one full oscillation in 1 s.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A seconds pendulum 'ticks' every second. Each swing from one extreme to the other takes 1 s, so the full period is T = 2 s.\nStatement I: $L = \\frac{gT^2}{4\\pi^2} = \\frac{9.8 \\times 4}{4\\pi^2}$ ≈ 0.99 m ≈ 1 m. This is correct.\nStatement II: one full oscillation takes 2 s, not 1 s. This is incorrect.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":94,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Four pendulums A, B, C and D are suspended from the same elastic support as shown in Fig. 14.1. A and C are of the same length, while B is smaller than A and D is larger than A. If A is given a transverse displacement,","image":"","options":["D will vibrate with maximum amplitude.","C will vibrate with maximum amplitude.","B will vibrate with maximum amplitude.","All the four will oscillate with equal amplitude."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The oscillating pendulum A drives the elastic support at its own frequency, $f = \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sqrt{g/L_A}$.\nThe other pendulums undergo forced oscillations at this frequency.\nC has the same length as A, so its natural frequency matches the driving frequency. Resonance occurs and C vibrates with the maximum amplitude.\nB (shorter) and D (longer) are off-resonance, so their amplitudes stay small.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 14 (MCQ Q14.7)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":95,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"The displacement of an oscillating quantity is given by $f(t) = A\\cos\\omega t$ with $\\omega = 4\\pi$ rad/s. What is the period of $f(t)$?","image":"","options":["0.25 s","0.5 s","1 s","2 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\omega = 4\\pi$ rad/s.\nFormula: the cosine repeats when $\\omega t$ increases by $2\\pi$, so $T = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$.\nSubstituting: $T = \\frac{2\\pi}{4\\pi}$.\nSo T = 0.5 s.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":96,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A block oscillates in SHM with an amplitude of 0.05 m and an angular frequency of 10 rad/s. What is the magnitude of its maximum acceleration?","image":"","options":["5 m/s^2","0.5 m/s^2","50 m/s^2","2.5 m/s^2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: A = 0.05 m, $\\omega$ = 10 rad/s\nFormula: $a_{max} = \\omega^2 A$\nSubstituting: $a_{max} = (10)^2 \\times 0.05$\nSo $a_{max} = 5$ m/s^2\nThe value 0.5 m/s^2 would come from wrongly using $\\omega A$, which is the maximum speed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":97,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"The function $x(t) = 3\\sin\\omega t + 4\\cos\\omega t$ is written in the form $x(t) = D\\sin(\\omega t + \\phi)$. Which of the following gives the correct values of $D$ and $\\phi$?","image":"","options":["$D = 5$, $\\phi = \\tan^{-1}(4/3)$","$D = 7$, $\\phi = \\tan^{-1}(4/3)$","$D = 5$, $\\phi = \\tan^{-1}(3/4)$","$D = 1$, $\\phi = \\tan^{-1}(3/4)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Expand: $D\\sin(\\omega t+\\phi) = D\\cos\\phi\\,\\sin\\omega t + D\\sin\\phi\\,\\cos\\omega t$.\nCompare coefficients: $D\\cos\\phi = 3$ and $D\\sin\\phi = 4$.\nAmplitude: $D = \\sqrt{3^2+4^2} = 5$. Amplitudes do not simply add, so 7 is wrong.\nPhase: $\\tan\\phi = 4/3$, i.e. $\\phi = \\tan^{-1}(B/A)$. Inverting this ratio is the common error.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":98,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A cylindrical cork of base area A, height h and density $\\rho$ floats upright in a liquid of density $\\rho_l$. It is depressed slightly and released (ignore viscosity). How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The period is independent of the base area A.\n(ii) The period equals $2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{d}{g}}$, where d is the depth of cork immersed at equilibrium.\n(iii) Using a denser liquid increases the period.\n(iv) The restoring force for an extra depression y has magnitude $A\\rho_l g y$.\n(v) Pressing the cork deeper initially (larger amplitude, still small) increases the period.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: A cancels, giving $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{h\\rho}{\\rho_l g}}$.\n(ii) Correct: flotation gives $d\\rho_l = h\\rho$, so $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{d}{g}}$.\n(iii) Incorrect: a larger $\\rho_l$ makes T smaller.\n(iv) Correct: the extra buoyant force is $A y\\rho_l g$, directed upward.\n(v) Incorrect: the period of SHM does not depend on amplitude.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":99,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When the bob of an oscillating simple pendulum passes through its mean position, the tension in the string equals the weight mg of the bob.\nReason (R): At any position, the net radial force $T - mg\\cos\\theta$ on the bob provides the centripetal acceleration needed for its motion along the circular arc.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"R is true. The bob moves on a circle of radius L, so $T - mg\\cos\\theta = \\frac{mv^2}{L}$.\nAt the mean position, $\\theta = 0$ and the speed is maximum (non-zero).\nSo $T = mg + \\frac{mv^2}{L}$, which is greater than mg. Hence A is false.\nT equals mg only if the bob is at rest at the mean position.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":100,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (function of time) with List-II (its period).\nList-I:\n(A) $3\\cos(\\pi/4 - 2\\omega t)$\n(B) $\\sin^2(\\omega t/2)$\n(C) $\\sin 3\\omega t + \\cos 3\\omega t$\n(D) $\\sin\\omega t + \\sin(\\omega t/2)$\nList-II:\n(I) $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$\n(II) $\\frac{\\pi}{\\omega}$\n(III) $\\frac{4\\pi}{\\omega}$\n(IV) $\\frac{2\\pi}{3\\omega}$","image":"","options":["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $\\cos(2\\omega t - \\pi/4)$ has angular frequency $2\\omega$, so period $\\pi/\\omega$ → II. \n(B) $\\sin^2(\\omega t/2) = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\cos\\omega t$, so period $2\\pi/\\omega$ → I. \n(C) This equals $\\sqrt{2}\\sin(3\\omega t + \\pi/4)$, so period $2\\pi/3\\omega$ → IV. \n(D) The periods are $2\\pi/\\omega$ and $4\\pi/\\omega$. The smallest common interval is $4\\pi/\\omega$ → III. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":101,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"The displacement of a particle is given by $x = 6\\sin\\omega t + 8\\cos\\omega t$ (x in cm). The motion can be written as $x = D\\sin(\\omega t + \\phi)$. What is the value of D?","image":"","options":["10 cm","14 cm","2 cm","7.07 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: A = 6 cm, B = 8 cm.\nFormula: $D = \\sqrt{A^2 + B^2}$.\nSubstituting: $D = \\sqrt{36 + 64} = \\sqrt{100}$.\nSo D = 10 cm. Adding the amplitudes directly (14 cm) is wrong, because the two terms are $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ out of phase.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":102,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A block attached to a spring of spring constant 400 N/m oscillates on a frictionless horizontal surface with an amplitude of 5 cm. What is the total mechanical energy of the oscillator?","image":"","options":["0.5 J","1.0 J","10 J","0.25 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: k = 400 N/m, A = 5 cm = 0.05 m.\nFormula: $E = \\frac{1}{2}kA^2$\nSubstituting: $E = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 400 \\times (0.05)^2 = 200 \\times 0.0025$\nSo E = 0.5 J. Using A = 5 without converting to metres, or dropping the factor ½, gives the wrong options.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":103,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following motions is periodic but NOT oscillatory?","image":"","options":["The pendulum of a wall clock","A stone tied to a string and whirled in a horizontal circle with constant angular speed","The piston of a steam engine moving back and forth","A boat tossing up and down on a river"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An oscillatory motion is a periodic to-and-fro motion about a mean (equilibrium) position.\nThe pendulum, the piston and the tossing boat all move to and fro about a mean position, so they are oscillatory.\nUniform circular motion repeats after every revolution, so it is periodic. But the stone goes round and round, not back and forth about an equilibrium point, so it is not oscillatory.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":104,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"For a particle in SHM described by $x = A\\cos(\\omega t + \\phi)$, what is the phase difference between its acceleration and its displacement?","image":"","options":["Zero","$\\frac{\\pi}{2}$","$\\pi$","$\\frac{\\pi}{4}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The acceleration in SHM is $a = -\\omega^2 A\\cos(\\omega t + \\phi) = -\\omega^2 x$.\nThe minus sign means a is always opposite to x, so acceleration and displacement are in antiphase. The phase difference is $\\pi$.\n$\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ is the tempting wrong choice. That is the phase difference between velocity and displacement, not between acceleration and displacement.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":105,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A particle acted upon by a force $F = -kx + bx^3$ (with b small but non-zero) is not a linear harmonic oscillator.\nReason (R): A linear harmonic oscillator is one in which the restoring force is linearly proportional to the displacement from the mean position.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: NCERT calls a particle under F = −kx a linear harmonic oscillator because the force is linearly proportional to x.\nA is true: the extra $bx^3$ term makes the force non-linear in x, so the system is a non-linear oscillator.\nA follows directly from the definition in R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":106,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The frequency of a periodic motion need not be an integer.\n(ii) The period is the largest time interval after which the motion repeats itself.\n(iii) Every oscillatory motion is periodic.\n(iv) Halley's comet reappears about every 76 years, so its frequency is of the order of $10^{-10}$ Hz.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT states that ν is not necessarily an integer (e.g., 1.25 Hz).\n(ii) Incorrect: the period is the smallest interval after which the motion repeats.\n(iii) Correct: every oscillatory motion is periodic, although the converse is not true.\n(iv) Correct: $\\frac{1}{76 \\times 3.15 \\times 10^{7}\\text{ s}} \\approx 4 \\times 10^{-10}$ Hz.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":107,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"The acceleration a of a particle is related to its displacement x from a fixed point as follows (all constants in SI units):\n(i) $a = -5x$\n(ii) $a = 3x$\n(iii) $a = -2x^2$\n(iv) $a = -\\pi^2 x$\n(v) $a = -4x^3$\n(vi) $a = -0.5x$\nHow many of these relations represent simple harmonic motion?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"SHM requires $a = -\\omega^2 x$. The acceleration must be linear in x and directed opposite to x.\n(i) SHM, with $\\omega^2 = 5$.\n(ii) Not SHM: a is along x, so it is not restoring.\n(iii) and (v) Not SHM: these are non-linear in x.\n(iv) SHM, with $\\omega = \\pi$.\n(vi) SHM, with $\\omega^2 = 0.5$.\nSo 3 relations represent SHM.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":108,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle performs SHM with an amplitude of 8 cm. At what distance from the mean position are its kinetic energy and potential energy equal?","image":"","options":["4.00 cm","5.66 cm","2.83 cm","6.93 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $A = 8$ cm, and the condition $K = U$.\nFormula: if $K = U$, then $U = \\frac{E}{2}$, so $\\frac{1}{2}kx^2 = \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}kA^2$. This gives $x = \\frac{A}{\\sqrt{2}}$.\nSubstituting: $x = \\frac{8}{1.414}$.\nSo x ≈ 5.66 cm. At $x = A/2 = 4$ cm, U is only $E/4$, so option (1) fails.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":109,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle executes SHM according to $x = 0.04\\cos(5t)$, where x is in metres and t is in seconds. What is the maximum speed of the particle?","image":"","options":["0.2 m/s","1.0 m/s","0.04 m/s","0.8 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A = 0.04$ m and $\\omega = 5$ rad/s.\nFormula: the velocity amplitude is $v_{max} = \\omega A$.\nSubstituting: $v_{max} = 5 \\times 0.04$.\nSo $v_{max} = 0.2$ m/s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":110,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle executes SHM $x = A\\cos\\omega t$ with period $T$ and total energy $E$. Match the instant in List-I with the kinetic energy of the particle at that instant in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) $t = \\frac{T}{12}$\n(B) $t = \\frac{T}{8}$\n(C) $t = \\frac{T}{6}$\n(D) $t = \\frac{T}{4}$\nList-II\n(i) $\\frac{E}{4}$\n(ii) $\\frac{E}{2}$\n(iii) $\\frac{3E}{4}$\n(iv) $E$","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-ii, C-i, D-iv","A-i, B-ii, C-iii, D-iv","A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-i, B-iii, C-ii, D-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The kinetic energy is $K = E\\sin^2\\omega t$, where $\\omega t = \\frac{2\\pi t}{T}$.\n(A) $\\omega t = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$, so $\\sin^2 = \\frac{1}{4}$ and K = $\\frac{E}{4}$ → (i).\n(B) $\\omega t = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$, so $\\sin^2 = \\frac{1}{2}$ and K = $\\frac{E}{2}$ → (ii).\n(C) $\\omega t = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$, so $\\sin^2 = \\frac{3}{4}$ and K = $\\frac{3E}{4}$ → (iii).\n(D) $\\omega t = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, so the particle is at the mean position and K = E → (iv).\nOption (1) is the tempting trap: it lists the potential energies, not the kinetic energies.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":111,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle executes SHM described by $x(t) = A\\cos(\\omega t + \\phi)$ with $\\omega = 2$ rad/s. At $t = 0$ its position is $x_0 = 3$ cm and its velocity is $v_0 = -8$ cm/s. What is the phase constant $\\phi$ (take $\\phi$ between $-180^\\circ$ and $180^\\circ$)?","image":"","options":["$37^\\circ$","$53^\\circ$","$-53^\\circ$","$127^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $x_0 = 3$ cm, $v_0 = -8$ cm/s, $\\omega = 2$ rad/s.\nFormula: $x_0 = A\\cos\\phi$ and $v_0 = -A\\omega\\sin\\phi$. These give $A = \\sqrt{x_0^2 + (v_0/\\omega)^2}$.\nSubstituting: $A = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = 5$ cm, so $\\cos\\phi = 3/5 = 0.6$.\nFrom $-8 = -(5)(2)\\sin\\phi$, we get $\\sin\\phi = +0.8$.\nBoth $\\sin\\phi$ and $\\cos\\phi$ are positive, so $\\phi$ lies in the first quadrant: $\\phi = 53^\\circ$.\nThe option $-53^\\circ$ comes from dropping the minus sign in the velocity formula.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":112,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A quantity varies with time as $f(t) = A\\cos(50\\pi t)$, where t is in seconds. What is the period of this function?","image":"","options":["0.04 s","0.02 s","25 s","0.08 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\omega = 50\\pi$ rad/s.\nFormula: $T = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$, because the cosine first repeats when its argument increases by $2\\pi$.\nSubstituting: $T = \\frac{2\\pi}{50\\pi} = \\frac{1}{25}$ s.\nSo T = 0.04 s. The value 0.02 s comes from wrongly using $T = \\frac{\\pi}{\\omega}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":113,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moves anticlockwise on a circle of radius A with constant angular speed $\\omega$. Its position vector makes an angle $\\phi$ with the +x axis at t = 0.\nStatement I: The projection of the particle on the y-axis, $y = A\\sin(\\omega t + \\phi)$, is also an SHM with amplitude A.\nStatement II: The projections on the x-axis and the y-axis differ in phase by $\\pi$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The y-projection is $A\\sin(\\omega t + \\phi)$, which is an SHM with the same amplitude A and the same $\\omega$.\nStatement II is incorrect. We can write $\\sin\\theta = \\cos(\\theta - \\pi/2)$, so the two projections differ in phase by $\\pi/2$, not $\\pi$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":114,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A spring balance has a scale reading from 0 to 40 kg, and the length of the scale is 16 cm. A body hung from the balance is pulled down slightly and released. It oscillates vertically with a period of 0.5 s. What is the weight of the body? (Take g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["158 N","15.8 N","63.3 N","253 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: a load of 40 kg × 10 m/s² = 400 N stretches the spring by 0.16 m; T = 0.5 s.\nStep 1: $k = \\frac{400}{0.16}$ = 2500 N/m.\nFormula: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{m}{k}}$, so $m = \\frac{kT^2}{4\\pi^2}$\nSubstituting: $m = \\frac{2500 \\times 0.25}{39.48}$ ≈ 15.8 kg.\nSo W = mg ≈ 158 N. The option 15.8 N wrongly uses the mass value as the weight.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":115,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A simple pendulum oscillates with small amplitude.\nStatement I: The tension in the string is maximum when the bob passes through the mean position.\nStatement II: At the mean position the tangential force $mg\\sin\\theta$ is zero, so the bob has zero acceleration there.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The radial equation is $T - mg\\cos\\theta = \\frac{mv^2}{L}$, so $T = mg\\cos\\theta + \\frac{mv^2}{L}$.\nStatement I is correct. At the mean position $\\cos\\theta = 1$ (its maximum) and the speed is maximum, so T is maximum.\nStatement II is incorrect. The tangential acceleration is zero at the mean position, but the bob still moves on a circle. It therefore has a radial (centripetal) acceleration $v^2/L$, which is non-zero.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":116,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions of time represent periodic motion? ($\\omega$ is a positive constant)\n(i) $\\cos 2\\omega t - \\sin 2\\omega t$\n(ii) $e^{-2\\omega t}$\n(iii) $\\cos^2 \\omega t$\n(iv) $\\omega t + \\sin \\omega t$\n(v) $\\sin \\omega t + \\cos 4\\omega t$\n(vi) $\\ln(1+\\omega t)$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Periodic: it equals $\\sqrt{2}\\cos(2\\omega t+\\frac{\\pi}{4})$, with period $\\frac{\\pi}{\\omega}$.\n(ii) Not periodic: it decreases monotonically towards zero.\n(iii) Periodic: it equals $\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2\\omega t$, with period $\\frac{\\pi}{\\omega}$.\n(iv) Not periodic: the $\\omega t$ term grows without bound.\n(v) Periodic: one frequency is an integral multiple of the other, so the period is $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$.\n(vi) Not periodic: it increases monotonically.\nSo three functions are periodic.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":117,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A reference particle revolves uniformly on a circle of radius 0.1 m with a period of 4 s. What is the maximum speed of its projection on a diameter?","image":"","options":["0.157 m/s","0.314 m/s","0.0785 m/s","0.628 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: A = 0.1 m, T = 4 s\nFormula: $v_{max} = \\omega A$, with $\\omega = \\frac{2\\pi}{T}$\nSubstituting: $\\omega = \\frac{2\\pi}{4} = 1.571$ rad/s, so $v_{max} = 1.571 \\times 0.1$\nSo the maximum speed = 0.157 m/s. This equals the speed of the reference particle, reached when P′ passes through the centre.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":118,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a particle moves uniformly on a circle, its projections on the x-axis and the y-axis both execute SHM of the same amplitude, differing in phase by $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\nReason (R): The force on a particle in linear SHM is the same as the centripetal force that keeps the reference particle in uniform circular motion.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The projections are $x = A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)$ and $y = A\\sin(\\omega t+\\phi)$. Both are SHMs of amplitude A, and they differ in phase by $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\nR is false. NCERT stresses that the restoring force in linear SHM is very different from the centripetal force in circular motion. The SHM force acts along a line towards the mean position and its magnitude varies. The centripetal force has constant magnitude and always points to the centre.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":119,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A body capable of oscillating is placed exactly at its equilibrium position and left at rest. According to NCERT, what happens to it?","image":"","options":["It starts oscillating with a small amplitude on its own","It slowly drifts away because the restoring force is maximum there","It remains at rest there indefinitely, since no net external force acts on it","It oscillates with gradually increasing amplitude"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"At the equilibrium position, no net external force acts on the body.\nWith zero net force and zero initial velocity, the body stays at rest there forever.\nOscillations begin only when the body is given a small displacement. A force then comes into play that pulls it back towards equilibrium.\nOption (2) is wrong because the restoring force is zero at equilibrium, not maximum.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":120,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A 2 kg mass attached to a spring of force constant 800 N/m oscillates horizontally on a frictionless floor. Its time period is approximately:","image":"","options":["0.314 s","3.14 s","0.0314 s","0.628 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 2 kg, k = 800 N/m.\nFormula: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{m}{k}}$\nSubstituting: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{800}} = 2\\pi \\times \\frac{1}{20}$\nSo T = $\\frac{\\pi}{10}$ ≈ 0.314 s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":121,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions of time represent simple harmonic motion? ($\\omega$ is a positive constant.)\n(i) $\\sin\\omega t - \\cos\\omega t$\n(ii) $\\sin^3\\omega t$\n(iii) $\\cos\\omega t + \\cos 3\\omega t + \\cos 5\\omega t$\n(iv) $5\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3} - 4\\omega t\\right)$\n(v) $\\sin\\omega t\\,\\cos\\omega t$\n(vi) $e^{-\\omega t}\\sin\\omega t$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) SHM: it equals $\\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(\\omega t - \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$.\n(ii) Not SHM: it equals $\\frac{3\\sin\\omega t - \\sin 3\\omega t}{4}$, two frequencies, so periodic but not SHM.\n(iii) Not SHM: three frequencies; periodic with period $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$.\n(iv) SHM: it equals $5\\cos\\left(4\\omega t - \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$, a single sinusoid.\n(v) SHM: it equals $\\frac{1}{2}\\sin 2\\omega t$.\n(vi) Not SHM: the amplitude keeps decaying, so it is non-periodic.\nSo three functions, (i), (iv) and (v), represent SHM.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":122,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Uniform circular motion is periodic but not oscillatory.\nReason (R): In uniform circular motion, the body does not move to and fro about an equilibrium position, although its motion repeats at regular intervals.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that circular motion is periodic but not oscillatory.\nR is true. The motion repeats after every revolution, which makes it periodic. However, there is no back-and-forth motion about a mean (equilibrium) position.\nOscillatory motion specifically means to-and-fro motion about a mean position. So R is exactly why A holds, and R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":123,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a block oscillating on an ideal spring, doubling the amplitude doubles the time period.\nReason (R): For a block oscillating on an ideal spring, doubling the amplitude doubles the maximum restoring force.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A is false: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{m}{k}}$ depends only on m and k. The period of SHM does not depend on amplitude.\nR is true: the maximum restoring force is $F_{max} = kA$, so it doubles when A doubles.\nBoth the restoring force and the distance to be covered double, so the period stays the same.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":124,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following functions of time represents a non-periodic motion? ($\\omega$ is a positive constant)","image":"","options":["$\\sin 2\\omega t + \\cos\\omega t$","$e^{-\\omega t}$","$\\sin\\omega t\\,\\cos\\omega t$","$3\\cos(\\omega t + \\frac{\\pi}{3})$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$e^{-\\omega t}$ decreases steadily with time and tends to zero as $t \\to \\infty$. It never repeats a value, so it is non-periodic.\n(1) A sum of terms with periods $\\frac{\\pi}{\\omega}$ and $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$ is periodic with period $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$.\n(3) $\\sin\\omega t\\cos\\omega t = \\frac{1}{2}\\sin 2\\omega t$, which is periodic.\n(4) This is a simple sinusoid, so it is periodic.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":125,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A 1 kg block fastened to a spring of spring constant 50 N/m is pulled 10 cm from its equilibrium position on a frictionless surface and released from rest. What is its kinetic energy when it is 6 cm from the mean position?","image":"","options":["0.16 J","0.09 J","0.25 J","0.07 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: k = 50 N/m, A = 0.10 m, x = 0.06 m.\nFormula: $K = E - U = \\frac{1}{2}k(A^2 - x^2)$\nSubstituting: $K = \\frac{1}{2}\\times 50\\times(0.01 - 0.0036) = 25 \\times 0.0064$\nSo K = 0.16 J. Note that 0.09 J is the potential energy at this point and 0.25 J is the total energy.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":126,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A cylindrical cork of height 10 cm and density 250 kg m$^{-3}$ floats upright in water of density 1000 kg m$^{-3}$. It is pressed down slightly and released. Ignoring viscous damping, find the period of its vertical oscillations. (Take g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["0.314 s","0.628 s","0.157 s","1.257 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: h = 0.1 m, $\\rho$ = 250 kg m$^{-3}$, $\\rho_l$ = 1000 kg m$^{-3}$, g = 10 m/s$^2$.\nFormula: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{h\\rho}{\\rho_l g}}$\nSubstituting: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{0.1 \\times 250}{1000 \\times 10}} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{0.0025} = 2\\pi \\times 0.05$\nSo T ≈ 0.314 s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":127,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle executes SHM according to $x = A\\cos\\omega t$ with period T. Match the instant in List-I with the state of the particle in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) t = 0\n(b) t = T/4\n(c) t = T/2\n(d) t = 3T/4\nList-II\n(i) x = 0, v = $+\\omega A$\n(ii) x = +A, a = $-\\omega^2 A$\n(iii) x = 0, v = $-\\omega A$\n(iv) x = −A, a = $+\\omega^2 A$","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Use $x = A\\cos\\omega t$, $v = -\\omega A\\sin\\omega t$ and $a = -\\omega^2 x$, with $\\omega t = \\frac{2\\pi t}{T}$.\n(a) $\\omega t = 0$: x = A, so a = $-\\omega^2 A$ → (ii)\n(b) $\\omega t = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$: x = 0, v = $-\\omega A$ → (iii)\n(c) $\\omega t = \\pi$: x = −A, so a = $+\\omega^2 A$ → (iv)\n(d) $\\omega t = \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$: x = 0, v = $+\\omega A$ → (i)\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":128,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the force in simple harmonic motion is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The restoring force is always directed towards the mean position.","For a block on a spring, $k = m\\omega^2$, so that $\\omega = \\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}}$.","The restoring force has its maximum magnitude at the mean position, where the speed is also maximum.","If the force contains small additional terms proportional to $x^2$, $x^3$, etc., the oscillator is called non-linear."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: F = −kx, so the force always points towards x = 0.\n(2) Correct: combining F = ma with $a = -\\omega^2 x$ gives $k = m\\omega^2$.\n(3) Incorrect: |F| = k|x|. The force is zero at the mean position and maximum at the extremes. Only the speed is maximum at the mean position.\n(4) Correct: NCERT calls such systems non-linear oscillators.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":129,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about energy in linear SHM are correct?\n(i) The total energy is proportional to the square of the amplitude.\n(ii) Both kinetic and potential energy reach their maximum value twice in each period.\n(iii) On an energy versus displacement graph, U is a parabola, K is an inverted parabola, and E is a horizontal line.\n(iv) If the frequency of a given mass is doubled while keeping the amplitude the same, the total energy doubles.\n(v) With $U = \\frac{1}{2}kx^2$, the potential energy becomes negative when the displacement is negative.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $E = \\frac{1}{2}kA^2$.\n(ii) Correct: K and U both have period T/2, so each peaks twice per period.\n(iii) Correct: $U \\propto x^2$, $K \\propto (A^2 - x^2)$, and E is constant.\n(iv) Incorrect: $E = \\frac{1}{2}m\\omega^2A^2$, so doubling ω makes E four times as large.\n(v) Incorrect: $x^2 \\ge 0$, so U is never negative.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":130,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle P revolves anticlockwise on a circle of radius 3 cm centred at the origin, with a period of 6 s. At t = 0, its position vector OP makes an angle of $60^\\circ$ with the +x axis. The x-projection of OP (in cm, t in s) is:","image":"","options":["$x = 3\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{3} + \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$","$x = 3\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{3} - \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$","$x = 3\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{6} + \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$","$x = 3\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{3} + \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Angular speed: $\\omega = \\frac{2\\pi}{6} = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$ rad/s.\nThe motion is anticlockwise, so the angle increases with time: $\\theta(t) = \\frac{\\pi t}{3} + \\frac{\\pi}{3}$.\nThe x-projection is $A\\cos\\theta = 3\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{3} + \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$.\nOption (2) has the wrong initial phase. Option (3) uses $\\pi/T$ instead of $2\\pi/T$. Option (4) is the y-projection.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":131,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The shadow of a ball whirled in a horizontal circle at constant angular speed, cast on a wall perpendicular to the plane of the circle, executes simple harmonic motion.\nReason (R): The force acting on a particle in linear SHM is the same as the centripetal force needed to keep the reference particle in uniform circular motion.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The shadow on the wall is the projection of uniform circular motion onto a diameter of the circle. Such a projection follows $x = A\\cos(\\omega t + \\phi)$, which is SHM.\nR is false. NCERT points out that the force on a particle in linear SHM is very different from the centripetal force on the reference particle.\nThe SHM force acts along a line and varies as $-kx$. The centripetal force has constant magnitude and always points towards the centre of the circle.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":132,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle executes SHM along the x-axis with angular frequency $2\\pi$ rad/s. At t = 0 its position is +3 cm and its velocity is $+8\\pi$ cm/s. What is the magnitude of its maximum acceleration?","image":"","options":["1.97 m/s$^2$","1.18 m/s$^2$","0.31 m/s$^2$","2.76 m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\omega = 2\\pi$ rad/s, $x_0 = 3$ cm, $v_0 = 8\\pi$ cm/s.\nFrom $x = A\\cos\\phi$ and $v = -\\omega A\\sin\\phi$: $A = \\sqrt{x_0^2 + (v_0/\\omega)^2}$.\nSubstituting: $A = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = 5$ cm = 0.05 m.\nMaximum acceleration: $a_m = \\omega^2 A = 4\\pi^2 \\times 0.05 \\approx 1.97$ m/s$^2$.\nUsing $x_0$ in place of A gives 1.18 m/s$^2$, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":133,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"The displacement of a particle is represented by the equation $y = \\sin^3 \\omega t$. The motion is","image":"","options":["non-periodic.","periodic but not simple harmonic.","simple harmonic with period $2\\pi/\\omega$.","simple harmonic with period $\\pi/\\omega$."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Identity: $\\sin^3\\omega t = \\frac{3\\sin\\omega t - \\sin 3\\omega t}{4}$.\nThis is a sum of two SHMs with angular frequencies $\\omega$ and $3\\omega$. Both repeat after $2\\pi/\\omega$, so the motion is periodic.\nIt is not SHM: $\\frac{d^2y}{dt^2} = \\frac{-3\\omega^2\\sin\\omega t + 9\\omega^2\\sin 3\\omega t}{4}$, which is not of the form $-k\\,y$.\nSo the motion is periodic but not simple harmonic.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 14 (MCQ Q14.2)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":134,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A doctor counts a patient's pulse as 72 beats in one minute. Treating the heartbeat as a periodic motion, what is its time period?","image":"","options":["0.83 s","1.2 s","0.72 s","1.67 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 72 beats in 60 s.\nFormula: $\\nu = \\frac{\\text{number of repetitions}}{\\text{time}}$ and $T = \\frac{1}{\\nu}$\nSubstituting: $\\nu = \\frac{72}{60} = 1.2$ Hz, so $T = \\frac{1}{1.2}$ s\nSo T ≈ 0.83 s. The value 1.2 s is the frequency in Hz, not the period.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":135,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Motion of an oscillating liquid column in a U-tube is","image":"","options":["periodic but not simple harmonic.","non-periodic.","simple harmonic and time period is independent of the density of the liquid.","simple harmonic and time-period is directly proportional to the density of the liquid."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Displace one liquid level by $y$. The level difference is then $2y$.\nRestoring force: $F = -(2\\rho A g)\\,y$, which is proportional to $-y$, so the motion is SHM.\nMass of the column: $m = \\rho A L$.\nPeriod: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{\\rho A L}{2\\rho A g}} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{L}{2g}}$.\nThe density $\\rho$ cancels, so $T$ is independent of the density of the liquid.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 14 (MCQ Q14.4)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":136,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A light spring of force constant 800 N m$^{-1}$ lies on a smooth horizontal table. Each of its two ends is attached to a block of mass 1 kg, and neither end is fixed. The two blocks are pulled apart symmetrically, stretching the spring, and then released together. What is the period of oscillation of the blocks?","image":"","options":["0.157 s","0.222 s","0.314 s","0.111 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: k = 800 N m$^{-1}$, m = 1 kg at each end.\nNo external horizontal force acts, so by symmetry the midpoint of the spring stays at rest.\nEach block therefore oscillates on half the spring. A half-spring has force constant 2k = 1600 N m$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{m}{2k}}$\nSubstituting: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{1600}} = \\frac{2\\pi}{40}$\nSo T = 0.157 s. The value 0.222 s wrongly treats one end as fixed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":137,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle revolves clockwise on a circle of radius B centred at the origin, with a period of 8 s. At t = 0, it is on the positive y-axis. The x-projection of its position vector as a function of time (t in s) is:","image":"","options":["$x = B\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right)$","$x = -B\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right)$","$x = B\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right)$","$x = B\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{8}\\right)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Initial angle with the +x axis: $\\pi/2$. Angular speed: $\\omega = \\frac{2\\pi}{8} = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$ rad/s.\nThe motion is clockwise, so the angle decreases: $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\frac{\\pi t}{4}$.\nThen $x = B\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right) = B\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right)$.\nCheck: moving clockwise from the +y axis, the particle first goes towards +x, so x increases from zero. Option (2) would give the anticlockwise case.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":138,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a block oscillating on a spring on a frictionless surface, the total mechanical energy has the same value $\\frac{1}{2}kA^2$ at every instant.\nReason (R): During simple harmonic motion, the kinetic energy and the potential energy of the block each remain constant.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: $E = \\frac{1}{2}kA^2[\\cos^2(\\omega t+\\phi) + \\sin^2(\\omega t+\\phi)] = \\frac{1}{2}kA^2$, which is independent of time because the spring force is conservative.\nR is false: K and U each vary between zero and $\\frac{1}{2}kA^2$. Kinetic energy increases at the expense of potential energy and vice versa; only their sum is constant.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":139,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle executing SHM completes 20 oscillations in 8 s. What is its angular frequency?","image":"","options":["15.7 rad/s","2.5 rad/s","0.4 rad/s","31.4 rad/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 20 oscillations in 8 s.\nPeriod: $T = \\frac{8}{20} = 0.4$ s, so frequency $\\nu = 2.5$ Hz.\nFormula: $\\omega = \\frac{2\\pi}{T} = 2\\pi\\nu$\nSubstituting: $\\omega = 2\\pi \\times 2.5 = 5\\pi$\nSo $\\omega \\approx 15.7$ rad/s. The value 2.5 is the frequency, not the angular frequency.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":140,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"The displacement of a particle (in SI units) is $x = 0.2\\cos(\\pi t + \\frac{\\pi}{3})$. Including its sign, what is the velocity of the particle at t = 1 s?","image":"","options":["+0.544 m/s","−0.544 m/s","+0.314 m/s","+0.628 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: A = 0.2 m, $\\omega = \\pi$ rad/s, $\\phi = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$, t = 1 s\nFormula: $v = -\\omega A\\sin(\\omega t + \\phi)$\nSubstituting: $v = -0.2\\pi\\sin(\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{3}) = -0.2\\pi(-\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2})$\nSo v = +0.544 m/s, i.e. along +x.\nIgnoring the sign of $\\sin(\\frac{4\\pi}{3})$ gives the wrong value −0.544 m/s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":141,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following represent periodic motion?\n(i) A hydrogen molecule rotating about its centre of mass\n(ii) A freely suspended bar magnet displaced from the N-S direction and released\n(iii) An arrow released from a bow\n(iv) The rotation of the earth about its axis\n(v) A stone dropped from a cliff","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Periodic: the molecule returns to the same orientation after each rotation.\n(ii) Periodic: the magnet oscillates to and fro about the N-S direction.\n(iii) Not periodic: the arrow never returns to its starting position.\n(iv) Periodic: the rotation repeats every day.\n(v) Not periodic: the fall happens only once.\nSo 3 of the motions are periodic.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":142,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle performs SHM with an amplitude of 10 cm and an angular frequency of 4 rad/s. What is its speed when it is 6 cm from the mean position?","image":"","options":["32 cm/s","40 cm/s","24 cm/s","16 cm/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: A = 10 cm, $\\omega$ = 4 rad/s, x = 6 cm\nFormula: $v = \\omega\\sqrt{A^2 - x^2}$\nSubstituting: $v = 4\\sqrt{100 - 36} = 4 \\times 8$\nSo v = 32 cm/s\nThe value 40 cm/s is $v_{max}$, the speed at the mean position, not at x = 6 cm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":143,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The motion of a simple pendulum is simple harmonic only for small angular displacements.\nReason (R): For small $\\theta$, $\\sin\\theta \\approx \\theta$ (in radians), so the angular acceleration becomes $\\alpha = -\\frac{mgL}{I}\\theta$, which has the same form as $a = -\\omega^2 x$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the exact equation $\\alpha = -\\frac{mgL}{I}\\sin\\theta$ is not linear in $\\theta$, so the motion is not strictly SHM.\nR is true: $\\sin\\theta = \\theta - \\frac{\\theta^3}{3!} + \\dots$, and for small $\\theta$ the higher terms are negligible. This gives $\\alpha \\propto -\\theta$, which matches the SHM equation.\nR is exactly the reason why the small-angle restriction is needed, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":144,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A simple pendulum has a period of 2 s on Earth ($g = 10$ m/s$^2$). Its length is reduced to one-fourth, and it is taken to a planet where the acceleration due to gravity is 2.5 m/s$^2$. What is its new period for small oscillations?","image":"","options":["1 s","2 s","4 s","0.5 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: T = 2 s, g = 10 m/s$^2$, L' = L/4, g' = 2.5 m/s$^2$ = g/4.\nFormula: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{L}{g}}$.\nSubstituting: $T' = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{L/4}{g/4}} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{L}{g}} = T$.\nBoth length and g are divided by 4, so their ratio is unchanged.\nSo T' = 2 s.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":145,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle executes simple harmonic motion with a frequency of 2.5 Hz. What is its angular frequency?","image":"","options":["15.7 rad/s","2.5 rad/s","7.85 rad/s","0.4 rad/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\nu$ = 2.5 Hz \nFormula: $\\omega = 2\\pi\\nu$ \nSubstituting: $\\omega = 2\\pi \\times 2.5 = 5\\pi$ \nSo $\\omega \\approx$ 15.7 rad/s. \n0.4 is the period in seconds, not $\\omega$. 7.85 rad/s comes from wrongly using $\\pi\\nu$. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":146,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A simple pendulum of length 1.0 m hangs from the roof of a car. The car moves with a uniform speed of 10 m/s on a horizontal circular track of radius 10 m. Find the period of small oscillations of the pendulum about its equilibrium position. (g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["1.99 s","1.41 s","1.67 s","2.36 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: L = 1.0 m, v = 10 m/s, R = 10 m, g = 10 m/s$^2$.\nIn the car's frame, the bob feels g downward and the centrifugal acceleration $\\frac{v^2}{R}$ = 10 m/s$^2$ horizontally. These two are perpendicular.\nFormula: $g_{eff} = \\sqrt{g^2 + \\left(\\frac{v^2}{R}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{200}$ ≈ 14.14 m/s$^2$\nSubstituting: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{L}{g_{eff}}} = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{14.14}}$\nSo T ≈ 1.67 s. The value 1.41 s comes from wrongly adding the accelerations directly.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":147,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A simple pendulum has a period of 2.0 s on the surface of the Earth. It is taken to the Moon, where the acceleration due to gravity is one-sixth of that on Earth. What is its period on the Moon?","image":"","options":["12 s","4.9 s","0.82 s","2.0 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $T_E$ = 2.0 s, $g_M = \\frac{g_E}{6}$.\nFormula: $T \\propto \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{g}}$, so $\\frac{T_M}{T_E} = \\sqrt{\\frac{g_E}{g_M}}$\nSubstituting: $T_M = 2.0 \\times \\sqrt{6}$\nSo $T_M$ ≈ 4.9 s. The answer 12 s wrongly takes T proportional to 1/g instead of $1/\\sqrt{g}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":148,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"The SHM $x = 2\\sin(\\omega t + \\pi/6)$ is rewritten in the form $x = 2\\cos(\\omega t + \\phi)$. The value of $\\phi$ is:","image":"","options":["$-\\frac{\\pi}{3}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{3}$","$\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$","$-\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Use the identity $\\sin\\theta = \\cos(\\theta - \\pi/2)$. \nSo $2\\sin(\\omega t + \\pi/6) = 2\\cos(\\omega t + \\pi/6 - \\pi/2) = 2\\cos(\\omega t - \\pi/3)$. \nTherefore $\\phi = -\\pi/3$. \n$+2\\pi/3$ comes from wrongly adding $\\pi/2$ instead of subtracting it. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":149,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle executes SHM with amplitude A, angular frequency $\\omega$ and period T. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) At $x = \\frac{A}{2}$, the kinetic energy is $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the total energy.\n(ii) At $x = \\frac{A}{2}$, the speed is $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\omega A$.\n(iii) Kinetic and potential energies are equal at $x = \\frac{A}{\\sqrt{2}}$.\n(iv) The minimum time to go from the mean position to $x = \\frac{A}{2}$ is $\\frac{T}{6}$.\n(v) At $x = \\frac{A}{2}$, the magnitude of acceleration is $\\frac{\\omega^2 A}{2}$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $U = \\frac{1}{2}k\\frac{A^2}{4} = \\frac{E}{4}$, so $K = \\frac{3E}{4}$.\n(ii) Correct: $v = \\omega\\sqrt{A^2 - \\frac{A^2}{4}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\omega A$.\n(iii) Correct: setting $U = \\frac{E}{2}$ gives $x^2 = \\frac{A^2}{2}$, so $x = \\frac{A}{\\sqrt{2}}$.\n(iv) Incorrect: with $x = A\\sin\\omega t$, $\\sin\\omega t = \\frac{1}{2}$ gives $\\omega t = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$, so $t = \\frac{T}{12}$.\n(v) Correct: $|a| = \\omega^2|x| = \\frac{\\omega^2 A}{2}$.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":150,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Every oscillatory motion is periodic, but not every periodic motion is oscillatory. Which of the following is periodic but NOT oscillatory?","image":"","options":["A freely suspended bar magnet turned slightly away from the N-S direction and released","A hydrogen molecule rotating steadily about its centre of mass","A small ball released near the lowest point inside a smooth hemispherical bowl","The mercury column in a U-tube after one side is pressed down and released"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Oscillatory motion is to-and-fro motion about an equilibrium (mean) position, driven by a restoring force.\n(1) The displaced magnet swings back and forth about the N-S line, so it oscillates.\n(2) The steady rotation of a hydrogen molecule repeats after each turn, so it is periodic. It never moves to and fro about a mean position, like circular motion, so it is not oscillatory.\n(3) The ball rolls back and forth about the bottom of the bowl, so it oscillates.\n(4) The mercury level rises and falls about its equilibrium level, so it oscillates.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":151,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The oscillation of a simple pendulum is simple harmonic only when its angular displacement is small.\nReason (R): For small $\\theta$ (in radians), $\\sin\\theta \\approx \\theta$, so the restoring torque becomes proportional to the angular displacement.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The restoring torque is $\\tau = -mgL\\sin\\theta$, which is not proportional to $\\theta$ in general.\nR is true. From the series $\\sin\\theta = \\theta - \\frac{\\theta^3}{3!} + ...$, small $\\theta$ gives $\\sin\\theta \\approx \\theta$, and the approximation holds well even up to about 20°.\nWith this approximation, $\\alpha = -\\frac{mgL}{I}\\theta$, which has the same form as $a = -\\omega^2 x$. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":152,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are examples of oscillatory motion?\n(i) A child rocking on a swing\n(ii) The piston of a steam engine\n(iii) The orbital motion of the planet Mercury around the Sun\n(iv) Atoms of a solid vibrating about their equilibrium positions\n(v) A stone whirled uniformly in a horizontal circle\n(vi) The membrane of a drum after being struck","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Oscillatory: to-and-fro motion about the mean position.\n(ii) Oscillatory: the piston moves back and forth.\n(iii) Not oscillatory: the orbit is periodic but there is no to-and-fro motion.\n(iv) Oscillatory: the atoms vibrate about their equilibrium positions.\n(v) Not oscillatory: uniform circular motion.\n(vi) Oscillatory: the membrane vibrates about its mean position.\nTotal = 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":153,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle performs linear SHM between two points P and Q that are 12 cm apart. The direction from P to Q is taken as positive. At a certain instant the particle is 2 cm from Q and moving towards P. The signs of its velocity, acceleration and the force on it are, respectively,","image":"","options":["negative, negative, negative","negative, positive, positive","positive, negative, negative","negative, negative, positive"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: The mean position is the midpoint, 6 cm from P. A point 2 cm from Q is therefore at $x = +4$ cm.\nStep 2: Velocity: the particle moves towards P, so v is negative.\nStep 3: Acceleration: $a = -\\omega^2 x$ with $x > 0$, so a is negative.\nStep 4: Force: $F = ma$, so F is also negative. The force points towards the mean position, independent of the direction of motion.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":154,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"The displacement of a particle is represented by the equation $y = 3\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4} - 2\\omega t\\right)$. The motion of the particle is","image":"","options":["simple harmonic with period $2\\pi/\\omega$.","simple harmonic with period $\\pi/\\omega$.","periodic but not simple harmonic.","non-periodic."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rewrite: $y = 3\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4} - 2\\omega t\\right) = 3\\cos\\left(2\\omega t - \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$, since cosine is an even function.\nThis is a single sinusoid, so $\\frac{d^2y}{dt^2} = -(2\\omega)^2 y$. The motion is SHM with angular frequency $2\\omega$.\nPeriod: $T = \\frac{2\\pi}{2\\omega} = \\frac{\\pi}{\\omega}$.\nTempting trap: option (1) wrongly takes the angular frequency as $\\omega$ instead of $2\\omega$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 14 (MCQ Q14.1)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":155,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For a particle in linear SHM, the entire mechanical energy is kinetic when the particle passes through the mean position.\nStatement II: The potential energy of a particle in SHM varies periodically with the same period T as its displacement.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. At x = 0, $U = \\frac{1}{2}kx^2 = 0$, so $E = K$.\nStatement II: Incorrect. $U = \\frac{1}{2}kA^2\\cos^2(\\omega t+\\phi)$. Since $\\cos^2$ repeats every π, U has period T/2, not T.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":156,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle executes SHM with an amplitude of 4 cm and a period of 0.5 s. What is its maximum speed?","image":"","options":["50.3 cm/s","25.1 cm/s","8.0 cm/s","100.5 cm/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: A = 4 cm, T = 0.5 s\nFormula: $v_{max} = \\omega A$, with $\\omega = \\frac{2\\pi}{T}$\nSubstituting: $\\omega = \\frac{2\\pi}{0.5} = 4\\pi$ rad/s, so $v_{max} = 4\\pi \\times 4 = 16\\pi$ cm/s\nSo $v_{max} \\approx 50.3$ cm/s\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":157,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, damped simple harmonic motion (damping constant b, mass m) is best described as:","image":"","options":["Strictly simple harmonic at all times, because the restoring force is still proportional to displacement","Not periodic at all, even over short time intervals","Not strictly simple harmonic; it is approximately so only for time intervals much less than $\\frac{2m}{b}$","Simple harmonic only for time intervals much greater than $\\frac{2m}{b}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Damping removes energy, so the amplitude keeps decreasing. The motion therefore does not exactly repeat.\nNCERT states that damped SHM is not strictly simple harmonic. It is approximately SHM only for times much less than $2m/b$, when little amplitude has been lost.\nOption (1) fails because the damping force spoils strict SHM, even though a restoring force is still present.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":158,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A body oscillates according to $x = 4\\cos\\left(\\pi t + \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$ in SI units. At $t = 1$ s, match each quantity in List-I with its value in List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Phase of the motion\n(b) Displacement\n(c) Velocity\n(d) Acceleration\nList-II:\n(i) $-2$ m\n(ii) $2\\pi^2$ m/s$^2$\n(iii) $\\frac{4\\pi}{3}$ rad\n(iv) $2\\sqrt{3}\\pi$ m/s","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-iii, b-i, c-ii, d-iv","a-i, b-iii, c-iv, d-ii","a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Phase $= \\pi(1) + \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{4\\pi}{3}$ rad, which is (iii).\n(b) $x = 4\\cos\\frac{4\\pi}{3} = 4(-\\frac{1}{2}) = -2$ m, which is (i).\n(c) $v = -\\omega A\\sin\\frac{4\\pi}{3} = -4\\pi(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) = 2\\sqrt{3}\\pi$ m/s, which is (iv).\n(d) $a = -\\omega^2 x = -\\pi^2(-2) = 2\\pi^2$ m/s$^2$, which is (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":159,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT equal to a frequency of 1 Hz?","image":"","options":["1 oscillation per second","1 $s^{-1}$","60 oscillations per minute","1 oscillation per minute"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"By definition, 1 Hz = 1 oscillation per second = 1 $s^{-1}$.\n60 oscillations per minute = 60/60 s = 1 oscillation per second, so it also equals 1 Hz.\n1 oscillation per minute = $\\frac{1}{60}$ Hz ≈ 0.017 Hz. This is not 1 Hz.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":160,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"For small oscillations, the time period of a simple pendulum does NOT depend on which of the following?","image":"","options":["Length of the string","Acceleration due to gravity at the place","Mass of the bob","Effective acceleration acting on the bob"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"From $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{I}{mgL}}$ with $I = mL^2$, the mass cancels and $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{L}{g}}$.\nT depends on L and on g. In an accelerating frame, g is replaced by the effective acceleration.\nThe mass of the bob does not appear in the final formula, so the period is independent of it.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":161,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"For a particle in SHM described by $x(t) = A\\cos(\\omega t + \\phi)$, match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) $A$\n(b) $\\omega$\n(c) $\\omega t + \\phi$\n(d) $\\phi$\nList-II:\n(i) Phase constant\n(ii) Time-dependent phase\n(iii) Magnitude of maximum displacement\n(iv) $2\\pi$ times the frequency","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) $A$ is the amplitude, the magnitude of maximum displacement, so it matches (iii).\n(b) $\\omega = \\frac{2\\pi}{T} = 2\\pi\\nu$, so it matches (iv).\n(c) $\\omega t + \\phi$ changes with time and is called the phase, so it matches (ii).\n(d) $\\phi$ is the value of the phase at $t = 0$, the phase constant, so it matches (i).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":162,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A block of mass 2 kg lies on a frictionless horizontal surface. It is connected between two fixed walls by two identical springs, one on each side, each of spring constant 100 N/m. Both springs are unstretched at equilibrium. When the block is displaced slightly along the line of the springs and released, its period of oscillation is about:","image":"","options":["0.889 s","0.628 s","0.444 s","1.257 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: m = 2 kg, k = 100 N/m for each spring.\nStep 1: For a displacement x, one spring stretches and the other compresses. Both push the block towards the mean position, so the net force is F = −2kx. The effective spring constant is 2k = 200 N/m.\nFormula: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{m}{2k}}$\nSubstituting: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{200}} = 2\\pi(0.1)$\nSo T ≈ 0.628 s. The value 0.889 s is the period with a single spring.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":163,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A spring balance has a scale that reads from 0 to 40 kg, and the length of this scale is 16 cm. A body hung from the balance is displaced slightly downward and released. It oscillates vertically with a period of 0.4 s. What is the weight of the body? (Take g = 10 m s$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["101 N","253 N","10.1 N","50.7 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: a 40 kg load (400 N) stretches the spring by 0.16 m, and T = 0.4 s.\nSpring constant: k = 400/0.16 = 2500 N m$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{m}{k}}$, so $m = \\frac{kT^2}{4\\pi^2}$.\nSubstituting: $m = \\frac{2500 \\times 0.16}{39.48} = 10.1$ kg.\nWeight: W = mg = 10.1 × 10 = 101 N. Do not stop at 10.1, which is the mass in kg.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":164,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle executes SHM with amplitude 10 cm and angular frequency 4 rad/s. What is its speed when it is 6 cm from the mean position?","image":"","options":["0.32 m/s","0.40 m/s","0.24 m/s","0.16 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A = 0.10$ m, $x = 0.06$ m, $\\omega = 4$ rad/s.\nFormula: $v = \\omega\\sqrt{A^2 - x^2}$.\nSubstituting: $v = 4\\sqrt{0.01 - 0.0036} = 4 \\times 0.08$.\nSo $v = 0.32$ m/s.\nNote: 0.40 m/s is $\\omega A$, the maximum speed at the mean position, not the speed at $x = 6$ cm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":165,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Match each function in List-I with its period in List-II ($\\omega$ is a positive constant).\nList-I\n(A) $\\cos 4\\omega t$\n(B) $\\sin\\omega t\\cos\\omega t$\n(C) $\\cos^2(\\omega t/2)$\n(D) $\\sin(\\omega t/2) + \\cos\\omega t$\nList-II\n(i) $\\frac{\\pi}{2\\omega}$\n(ii) $\\frac{\\pi}{\\omega}$\n(iii) $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$\n(iv) $\\frac{4\\pi}{\\omega}$","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-i, C-iii, D-iv","A-i, B-ii, C-iii, D-iv","A-i, B-iii, C-ii, D-iv","A-i, B-ii, C-iv, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) $T = \\frac{2\\pi}{4\\omega} = \\frac{\\pi}{2\\omega}$, so A-i.\n(B) $\\sin\\omega t\\cos\\omega t = \\frac{1}{2}\\sin 2\\omega t$, so $T = \\frac{\\pi}{\\omega}$, giving B-ii.\n(C) $\\cos^2(\\omega t/2) = \\frac{1}{2}(1+\\cos\\omega t)$, so $T = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$, giving C-iii.\n(D) The periods are $\\frac{4\\pi}{\\omega}$ and $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$. The first is a multiple of the second, so the sum repeats after $\\frac{4\\pi}{\\omega}$, giving D-iv.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":166,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The superposition of two SHMs along the same line, $x_1 = A\\sin\\omega t$ and $x_2 = B\\sin(\\sqrt{2}\\,\\omega t)$, is a periodic motion.\nStatement II: Any periodic function can be expressed as a superposition of sine and cosine functions of different periods with suitable coefficients.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. NCERT notes that a combination of two SHMs is periodic only if one frequency is an integral multiple of the other.\nHere the frequency ratio is $\\sqrt{2}$, which is irrational, so no common period exists.\nStatement II is correct: this is Fourier's theorem, which NCERT quotes.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":167,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"The displacement of a particle is $x = 3\\sin\\omega t + 4\\cos\\omega t$ (in cm). When this is written in the form $x = D\\sin(\\omega t + \\phi)$, the values of $D$ and $\\phi$ are","image":"","options":["$D = 5$ cm, $\\phi = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)$","$D = 7$ cm, $\\phi = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)$","$D = 5$ cm, $\\phi = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)$","$D = 1$ cm, $\\phi = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Compare with $A\\sin\\omega t + B\\cos\\omega t$, taking $A = D\\cos\\phi$ and $B = D\\sin\\phi$.\nHere $A = 3$ and $B = 4$.\nAmplitude: $D = \\sqrt{A^2 + B^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = 5$ cm. Amplitudes do not simply add, so 7 cm is wrong.\nPhase: $\\phi = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{B}{A}\\right) = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":168,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle moves along the x-axis with $x(t) = A\\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi t}{T}\\right)$. At $t = \\frac{3T}{4}$, the particle is","image":"","options":["at $x = -A$ and momentarily at rest","at $x = 0$ moving towards $-x$","at $x = +A$ and momentarily at rest","at $x = 0$ moving towards $+x$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Position: at $t = \\frac{3T}{4}$ the argument is $\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$, and $\\cos\\frac{3\\pi}{2} = 0$. So the particle is at the mean position.\nVelocity: $v = -A\\omega\\sin\\frac{3\\pi}{2} = +A\\omega$, which is positive.\nThe particle passed through $x = -A$ at $t = \\frac{T}{2}$ and is now returning towards $+x$ with maximum speed.\nThe tempting option '$x = 0$ moving towards $-x$' describes the instant $t = \\frac{T}{4}$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":169,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following functions of time are periodic? ($\\omega$ is a positive constant)\n(i) $\\sin\\omega t + \\cos(\\pi\\omega t)$\n(ii) $\\sin^2\\omega t$\n(iii) $e^{-\\omega t}\\sin\\omega t$\n(iv) $\\cos 2\\omega t + \\sin 6\\omega t$\n(v) $\\log(\\omega t) + \\sin\\omega t$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Not periodic: the frequencies are in the ratio $\\pi$, which is not an integer, so the two terms never repeat together.\n(ii) Periodic: $\\sin^2\\omega t = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2\\omega t$, with period $\\frac{\\pi}{\\omega}$.\n(iii) Not periodic: the decaying factor $e^{-\\omega t}$ prevents any value from repeating.\n(iv) Periodic: $6\\omega$ is an integral multiple of $2\\omega$, so the period is $\\frac{\\pi}{\\omega}$.\n(v) Not periodic: the $\\log$ term grows monotonically.\nTwo functions are periodic.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":170,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle of mass $m$ executes SHM $x = A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)$. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Velocity amplitude\n(B) Acceleration amplitude\n(C) Maximum restoring force\n(D) Total mechanical energy\nList-II\n(i) $\\omega A$\n(ii) $\\omega^2 A$\n(iii) $m\\omega^2 A$\n(iv) $\\frac{1}{2}m\\omega^2A^2$","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-i, C-iii, D-iv","A-i, B-iii, C-ii, D-iv","A-i, B-ii, C-iii, D-iv","A-i, B-ii, C-iv, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A) $v = -\\omega A\\sin(\\omega t+\\phi)$, so the maximum speed is $\\omega A$ → (i).\n(B) $a = -\\omega^2 x$, so the maximum acceleration is $\\omega^2 A$ → (ii).\n(C) $F = ma$, so the maximum force is $m\\omega^2 A$ → (iii).\n(D) $E = \\frac{1}{2}kA^2$ with $k = m\\omega^2$, so $E = \\frac{1}{2}m\\omega^2A^2$ → (iv).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-171","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":171,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II for a particle of mass m executing SHM of amplitude A.\nList-I\n(a) Angular frequency of a mass on a spring of constant k\n(b) Period of a mass held between two identical springs (constant k each) fixed to walls on either side\n(c) Force constant in terms of m and $\\omega$\n(d) Magnitude of maximum acceleration\nList-II\n(i) $m\\omega^2$\n(ii) $\\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}}$\n(iii) $\\omega^2 A$\n(iv) $2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{m}{2k}}$","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) The relation $k = m\\omega^2$ gives $\\omega = \\sqrt{k/m}$, so (a)-(ii).\n(b) The net force is −2kx, so $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{m/2k}$, giving (b)-(iv).\n(c) Comparing F = ma = $-m\\omega^2 x$ with F = −kx gives $k = m\\omega^2$, so (c)-(i).\n(d) From $a = -\\omega^2 x$, the acceleration is largest at x = ±A, so (d)-(iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-172","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":172,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"The swaying of a tree branch is usually called an oscillation, while the to-and-fro motion of a sitar string is usually called a vibration. As per NCERT, this difference in naming is based on:","image":"","options":["the amplitude of the motion","the frequency of the motion","whether a restoring force is present","whether the motion is damped"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT says there is no significant physical difference between oscillations and vibrations.\nBy usage, a low-frequency to-and-fro motion (such as a tree branch) is called an oscillation, and a high-frequency one (such as a musical string) is called a vibration.\nBoth motions have a restoring force and both are damped, so options (3) and (4) cannot be the basis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-173","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":173,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A ball bearing released from a point slightly above the lowest point of a smooth spherical bowl executes (nearly) simple harmonic motion.\nStatement II: The period of this motion increases if a heavier ball bearing of the same size is used.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. For a small displacement, the restoring force $mg\\sin\\theta \\approx mg\\theta$ is proportional to the displacement and directed towards the lowest point. This is like a simple pendulum of length R.\nStatement II is incorrect. Mass cancels in the equation of motion, so $T \\approx 2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{R}{g}}$ does not depend on m.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-174","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":174,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"In the study of oscillations, the term displacement means the change with time of any physical quantity under consideration. Which of the following is NOT a displacement variable in this sense?","image":"","options":["Angle made by the string of an oscillating simple pendulum with the vertical","Voltage across a capacitor in an AC circuit","Pressure variation at a point as a sound wave passes","Mass of the bob of an oscillating pendulum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT uses displacement for any physical property that changes with time during an oscillation.\nThe pendulum's angle from the vertical, the capacitor voltage in an AC circuit and the pressure variation in a sound wave all oscillate with time. So they all qualify.\nThe mass of the bob stays constant during the motion. It is not a time-varying oscillating quantity, so it cannot serve as a displacement variable.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-175","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":175,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A material medium can be pictured as a collection of a large number of coupled oscillators. The collective oscillations of these constituents show up as:","image":"","options":["damping","waves","uniform circular motion","non-periodic drift"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes any material medium as a large collection of coupled oscillators.\nThe collective oscillations of these constituents appear as waves. Examples are water waves and seismic waves, and NCERT also lists electromagnetic waves as examples of waves.\nDamping is the loss of oscillation energy through dissipative causes. It is not a collective effect, so option (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-176","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":176,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A displacement variable varies as $f(t) = \\sin(\\pi t) + \\sqrt{3}\\cos(\\pi t)$, with t in seconds. What is the earliest time $t > 0$ at which f(t) reaches its maximum value?","image":"","options":["1/6 s","1/3 s","1/2 s","5/6 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: A = 1, B = $\\sqrt{3}$, $\\omega = \\pi$ rad/s.\nFormula: $f = D\\sin(\\omega t + \\phi)$ with $D = \\sqrt{A^2 + B^2} = 2$ and $\\phi = \\tan^{-1}(\\sqrt{3}) = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$.\nSo $f = 2\\sin(\\pi t + \\frac{\\pi}{3})$.\nThe maximum occurs when $\\pi t + \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, which gives $t = \\frac{1}{6}$ s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-177","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":177,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle performs SHM along the x-axis according to $x = A\\cos(\\omega t + \\phi)$ with amplitude 2 cm and period 6 s. At $t = 0$ it is at $x = +1$ cm and moving in the $+x$ direction. After what minimum time does it first reach $x = +2$ cm?","image":"","options":["2 s","1 s","0.5 s","5 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $A = 2$ cm, $T = 6$ s, $x(0) = 1$ cm, $v(0) > 0$.\nFind the phase constant: $\\cos\\phi = \\frac{1}{2}$. Also $v = -A\\omega\\sin\\phi > 0$, so $\\sin\\phi < 0$. Hence $\\phi = -\\frac{\\pi}{3}$.\nCondition: $x = +A$ when the phase $\\omega t + \\phi = 0$, so the phase must increase by $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$.\nSubstituting: $t = \\frac{\\pi/3}{2\\pi/T} = \\frac{T}{6} = 1$ s.\nChoosing $\\phi = +\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ instead would wrongly give 5 s.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-178","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":178,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A block of mass 0.5 kg is attached to a spring of spring constant 200 N/m and oscillates on a smooth horizontal surface. What is the angular frequency of its simple harmonic motion?","image":"","options":["20 rad/s","10 rad/s","400 rad/s","40 rad/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 0.5 kg, k = 200 N/m.\nFormula: $\\omega = \\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}}$\nSubstituting: $\\omega = \\sqrt{\\frac{200}{0.5}} = \\sqrt{400}$\nSo $\\omega$ = 20 rad/s. The option 400 rad/s is $\\frac{k}{m}$ without taking the square root.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-179","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":179,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A block on a spring executes SHM described by $x = A\\cos\\omega t$. At the instant it is at $x = +A$, which option correctly gives its speed and acceleration?","image":"","options":["Speed zero; acceleration of magnitude $\\omega^2 A$ directed towards the mean position","Speed $\\omega A$; acceleration zero","Speed zero; acceleration zero","Speed zero; acceleration of magnitude $\\omega^2 A$ directed away from the mean position"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"At the extreme position $x = +A$, $\\cos\\omega t = 1$, so $\\sin\\omega t = 0$.\nVelocity $v = -\\omega A\\sin\\omega t = 0$, so the block is momentarily at rest.\nAcceleration $a = -\\omega^2 x = -\\omega^2 A$. Its magnitude is maximum, and the negative sign shows it points towards the mean position (x = 0).\nOption (4) fails because SHM acceleration is always directed towards the mean position.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-180","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":180,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"A particle executes SHM $x = A\\cos(\\omega t + \\phi)$ with period 1.2 s. At t = 0 it is at $x = +A/2$ and moving towards +x. After how much time does it first reach x = +A?","image":"","options":["0.2 s","0.1 s","0.4 s","0.3 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: T = 1.2 s, x(0) = A/2, v(0) > 0. \nFrom x(0): $\\cos\\phi = 1/2$, so $\\phi = \\pm\\pi/3$. \nFrom v(0): $v = -A\\omega\\sin\\phi > 0$ needs $\\sin\\phi < 0$, so $\\phi = -\\pi/3$. \nThe particle reaches x = +A when the phase becomes 0: $\\omega t = \\pi/3$, so $t = T/6$. \nSubstituting: t = 1.2/6 = 0.2 s. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-OSCILLATIONS-181","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Oscillations","qno":181,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Oscillations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The function $e^{-\\omega t}$ ($\\omega > 0$) cannot describe a periodic motion.\nReason (R): Any periodic function can be expressed as a superposition of sine and cosine functions of different time periods with suitable coefficients.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. $e^{-\\omega t}$ decreases monotonically and tends to zero as $t \\to \\infty$. It never repeats a value, so it is non-periodic.\nR is true. It is Fourier's theorem as stated in NCERT.\nHowever, R describes how periodic functions can be expressed. It does not explain why $e^{-\\omega t}$ fails to be periodic; monotonic decay explains that.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 13 · Oscillations","difficulty":"medium"}]});
